{{nihongo foot|'''Link'''|リンク|Rinku|lead=yes|group=lower-alpha}} is a [[Character (arts)|character]] and the [[protagonist]] of [[Nintendo]]'s video game franchise ''[[The Legend of Zelda]]''. He was created by Japanese video game designer [[Shigeru Miyamoto]]. Link was introduced as the hero of the original ''[[The Legend of Zelda (video game)|The Legend of Zelda]]'' video game in 1986 and has appeared in a total of [[List of The Legend of Zelda media#Main series|21 entries]] in the series, as well as a number of spin-offs. Common elements in the series include Link travelling through [[Hyrule]] whilst exploring dungeons, battling creatures, and solving puzzles until he eventually defeats the series' primary antagonist, [[Ganon]], and saves [[Princess Zelda]].

Throughout the series, Link has made multiple appearances in a variety of incarnations, but has been traditionally depicted in his signature green cap and tunic wielding a sword and shield. He has appeared as both a child and young adult of the [[elf]]-like [[Hylian]] race. Within ''Zelda'' mythology, Link is the soul of a legendary hero that throughout history is reincarnated within a seemingly ordinary boy or young man whenever evil arises. To defeat Ganon, Link usually obtains the mystical [[Master Sword]] or a similar legendary weapon obtained after completing many trials.

In addition to the main series, Link has appeared in other Nintendo media, including merchandise, [[The Legend of Zelda (manga)|comics and manga]], and an [[The Legend of Zelda (TV series)|animated television series]]. He is a prominent character in various spin-off games, including ''[[Hyrule Warriors]]'', ''[[Cadence of Hyrule]],'' ''[[Hyrule Warriors: Age of Calamity]], and [[Hyrule Warriors: Age of Imprisonment]].'' He has appeared in several other game franchises, including the ''[[Super Smash Bros.]]'' series, ''[[SoulCalibur II]],'' and ''[[Mario Kart 8]].'' He has also been referenced in other games, such as ''[[The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim]]''.

Alongside fellow Nintendo character [[Mario]], Link is one of the most recognisable characters in the [[video game industry]]. He has been instrumental in the establishment of the [[role-playing video game]] genre as the protagonist of the series, which has influenced numerous other video games with its concepts of [[open world]] and [[nonlinear gameplay]]. According to ''[[Guinness World Records]]'', Link is the most critically acclaimed video game playable character and the most ubiquitous action-adventure video game character. He was recognised by the ''[[Guinness World Records Gamer's Edition]]'' as the second best video game character after Mario. Critics have named him as one of the greatest and most influential video game characters of all time.

== Concept and creation ==
[[File:Linkamoto E3 2004.jpg|thumb|right|alt=Shigeru Miyamoto standing on a stage holding replicas of Link's sword and shield |[[Shigeru Miyamoto]], creator of Link, holding replicas of the character's signature [[Master Sword]] and shield at [[History of the Electronic Entertainment Expo#2004|Electronic Entertainment Expo 2004]]]]

=== Characterisation ===
Link's creator, [[Shigeru Miyamoto]], said that his concept of ''The Legend of Zelda'' was based on his childhood memories as well as books and movies that he and video game designer [[Takashi Tezuka]] had enjoyed, notably [[J. R. R. Tolkien]]'s ''[[The Lord of the Rings]]''. Miyamoto wanted people to have the opportunity to be heroes and created Link as a normal boy, with "a destiny to fight great evil".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Stanton|first=Richard|date=2023-04-06|title=How an 'Adventure Mario' project evolved to become The Legend Of Zelda|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/how-an-adventure-mario-project-evolved-to-become-the-legend-of-zelda/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230406170245/https://www.gamesradar.com/how-an-adventure-mario-project-evolved-to-become-the-legend-of-zelda/|archive-date=6 April 2023|access-date=2023-09-13|website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> On the origin of the character's name, Miyamoto said: "Link's name comes from the fact that originally, the fragments of the [[Triforce]] were supposed to be electronic chips. The game was to be set in both the past and the future and as the main character would travel between both and be the link between them, they called him Link". In the Nintendo book ''[[Hyrule Historia]]'' he said that the character is named Link because he, "connects people together" and, "he was supposed to spread the scattered energy of the world through the ages".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Byrd|first=Matthew|date=2021-05-24|title=The Legend of Zelda: What is the Origin of Link's Name?|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-link-name-origin-meaning-explained/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230608045800/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-link-name-origin-meaning-explained/|archive-date=8 June 2023|access-date=2021-10-08|website=Den of Geek}}</ref>

Link is the [[playable character]] who appears in every installment of ''The Legend of Zelda'' series. Although there are many iterations of Link, each with a similar role and appearance, they are not physically the same individual. The series spans thousands of years in Hyrule's history across [[The Legend of Zelda Timeline|''The Legend of Zelda'' fictional timeline]], so that each version of the character is a different Link, sometimes being a descendant of earlier incarnations or a spiritual reincarnation. In some games, such as ''[[Ocarina of Time]]'' and ''[[Majora's Mask]]'', the same version appears in both, continuing that character's individual story. ''[[Skyward Sword]]'', which was released in 2011, explained Link's incarnations by creating an origin story, in which the antagonist [[Demon King Demise]] curses the hero to be caught in an endless cycle of defeating evil in the form of [[Ganon]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Greenbaum|first=Aaron|date=2022-01-27|title=The Legend of Zelda: Is Every Link the Same Person?|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-every-link-same-person/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220129215742/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-every-link-same-person/|archive-date=29 January 2022|access-date=2023-09-13|website=Den of Geek}}</ref>

As no canonical game in ''The Legend of Zelda'' series to date has contained substantial spoken dialogue for Link, he is mostly a [[silent protagonist]], but not a mute character (except in ''[[The Legend of Zelda: Echoes of Wisdom]]'', where he is mute). His voice acting consists only of grunts, battle cries, and other sounds, although in ''[[The Wind Waker]]'', he can be heard saying, "Come on!" Despite lacking spoken dialogue, Link interacts with other characters through visual responses, such as nodding or shaking his head, reactions from side characters, or game text that expresses his dialogue. Series producer, [[Eiji Aonuma]] explained that a core aspect of Link's design is that players need to relate to him and put themselves in his shoes, while still playing as themselves. Link is silent to allow the player to interpret how he sounds, make decisions for the character and become the hero.<ref name=":9" />

=== Character design ===
Link's character design has been consistent, but it has evolved across the series. His [[Sprite (computer graphics)|sprite]] design was created by Tezuka. In an interview with ''[[Gamekult]]'', Miyamoto said that Tezuka used the [[Disney]] character [[Peter Pan (franchise)|Peter Pan]] as a source of inspiration when creating Link in order to make the character recognisable.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Madden|first=Orla|date=2012-11-05|title=The Legend of Zelda's Link Was Inspired By Walt Disney's Peter Pan|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2012/11/the_legend_of_zeldas_link_was_inspired_by_walt_disneys_peter_pan|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230529230111/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2012/11/the_legend_of_zeldas_link_was_inspired_by_walt_disneys_peter_pan|archive-date=29 May 2023|access-date=2021-10-06|website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> Due to the limited capabilities of the technology at the time, the development team was only able to use three colours and chose green as Link's signature colour, as the first game was mainly set within a forest environment. Link's sword and shield, long hat and ears were all created to make the character easily distinguishable.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Farokhmanesh|first=Megan|date=2012-11-04|title=Miyamoto wanted Link to be a recognizable character|url=https://www.polygon.com/2012/11/4/3598112/miyamoto-wanted-link-to-be-a-recognizable-character|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224803/https://www.polygon.com/2012/11/4/3598112/miyamoto-wanted-link-to-be-a-recognizable-character|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-08|website=Polygon}}</ref> For ''[[Ocarina of Time]]'', Miyamoto said that the team started by designing a grown-up model followed by a child model, but realised that both could be used in the game, "to tell the story of a boy growing up".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Serrels|first=Mark|date=2013-07-10|title=Why Are You Here? Shigeru Miyamoto And The Ocarina Of Time|url=https://www.kotaku.com.au/2013/07/why-are-you-here-miyamoto-and-the-ocarina-of-time/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231014224652/https://www.kotaku.com.au/2013/07/why-are-you-here-miyamoto-and-the-ocarina-of-time/|archive-date=14 October 2023|access-date=2022-04-12|website=Kotaku Australia}}</ref> Link's teenage appearance in ''Ocarina of Time'' was designed with the aim of making him more handsome, influenced by developer [[Yoshiaki Koizumi]]'s wife declaring, "all of Nintendo's characters have funny noses; don't you have any handsome ones?"<ref name=":10">{{Cite web|last=Frey|first=Angelica|date=2023-07-11|title=A Link between hairstyles|url=https://www.polygon.com/23779797/zelda-games-link-hairstyles|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230713210228/https://www.polygon.com/23779797/zelda-games-link-hairstyles|archive-date=13 July 2023|access-date=2023-07-12|website=Polygon}}</ref> Nintendo illustrator Yusuke Nakano said that Link's design in ''Ocarina of Time'' was based on a well-known American actor at the time of the game's development.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Richtmyer|first=Steven|date=December 20, 2020|title=Zelda: Ocarina of Time's Link Was (Probably) Based On Leonardo DiCaprio|url=https://screenrant.com/zelda-ocarina-time-link-leonardo-dicaprio/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404185440/https://screenrant.com/zelda-ocarina-time-link-leonardo-dicaprio/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=13 June 2021|website=Screen Rant}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Schreier|first=Jason|date=February 21, 2017|title= Which 'World-Famous Hollywood Actor' Is Ocarina of Time's Link Based On?|url= https://kotaku.com/which-world-famous-hollywood-actor-is-ocarina-of-times-1792586990|access-date=26 October 2024|website=Kotaku}}</ref>

Prior to ''[[The Wind Waker]]'' a cartoon version of Link was created by graphic designer Yoshiki Haruhana as a way to evolve the series, which was well received by the development team. According to designer Satoru Takizawa, this oversized anime-inspired design with huge eyes offered a richer variety of expression and provided a better way of representing the puzzles and mechanisms than a more photorealistic design.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Gera|first=Emily|date=2013-09-19|title=The Legend of Zelda: The Wind Waker designers contemplated having beams shoot out of Link's eyes|url=https://www.polygon.com/2013/9/19/4747570/the-legend-of-zelda-the-wind-waker-designers-contemplated-having|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602162416/https://www.polygon.com/2013/9/19/4747570/the-legend-of-zelda-the-wind-waker-designers-contemplated-having|archive-date=2 June 2023|access-date=2022-04-11|website=Polygon}}</ref> After many experiments, the development team had decided that [[cel-shading]] was the best option for expressing a young, energetic boy.<ref name=":6">{{Cite web|date=13 May 2004|title=E3 2004: Miyamoto and Aonuma on Zelda|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2004/05/13/e3-2004-miyamoto-and-aonuma-on-zelda|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404114947/https://www.ign.com/articles/2004/05/13/e3-2004-miyamoto-and-aonuma-on-zelda|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=19 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> Although the team loved the idea of basing the next ''Zelda'' game on a cartoon Link, Aonuma held off presenting it to Miyamoto until later in the game's development. When he did view it, Miyamoto reportedly cringed, believing that the game would not sell, but the team insisted that there was not enough time to develop a realistic art style.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Notis|first=Ari|date=2022-06-27|title=Miyamoto Couldn't Stand Wind Waker's Art Style At First|url=https://kotaku.com/the-legend-of-zelda-wind-waker-graphics-shigeru-miyamot-1849112926|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230331221047/https://kotaku.com/the-legend-of-zelda-wind-waker-graphics-shigeru-miyamot-1849112926|archive-date=31 March 2023|access-date=2023-09-20|website=Kotaku}}</ref> Toon Link eventually carved his own identity into the franchise by appearing in multiple entries in the series and being introduced as a playable character in ''[[Super Smash Bros.]]''<ref name=":7">{{Cite web|last=Otero|first=Jose|date=28 September 2013|title=The Artistic Legacy of the Legend of Zelda Wind Waker|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2013/09/28/the-artistic-legacy-of-the-legend-of-zelda-wind-waker-2|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404183315/https://www.ign.com/articles/2013/09/28/the-artistic-legacy-of-the-legend-of-zelda-wind-waker-2|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=19 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> Aonuma said that despite some initial outrage, gamers began to accept Toon Link, so ''[[Phantom Hourglass]]'' and ''[[Spirit Tracks]]'' were created with a similar artwork style.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web|last=Gaston|first=Martin|date=11 November 2013|title=Zelda director explains why people love Toon Link|url=https://www.gamespot.com/articles/zelda-director-explains-why-people-love-toon-link/1100-6415999/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404100908/https://www.gamespot.com/articles/zelda-director-explains-why-people-love-toon-link/1100-6415999/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-28|website=GameSpot}}</ref>

Following the response to ''The Wind Waker'', Nintendo reversed the art style for ''[[Twilight Princess]]'' in 2006, by depicting Link in a more realistic style with a darker, grittier tone.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|last=Marks|first=Tom|date=14 February 2019|title=Every Art Style Zelda Games Have Ever Had|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2019/02/14/every-art-style-the-legend-of-zelda-games-have-ever-had|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230605111224/https://www.ign.com/articles/2019/02/14/every-art-style-the-legend-of-zelda-games-have-ever-had|archive-date=5 June 2023|access-date=19 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> Nakano and Takizawa stated in ''[[The Legend of Zelda: Art & Artifacts|The Legend of Zelda: Art and Artifacts]]'' that they had planned to design him aged mid-to-late twenties and that he would be well built, but this was rejected because the team expected that fans wanted to see Link as he appeared in ''Ocarina of Time''.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Keefer|first=John|date=20 February 2017|title=The Legend of Zelda Art Book Reveals A 25-30-Year-Old Link|url=https://www.shacknews.com/article/99115/the-legend-of-zelda-art-book-reveals-a-25-30-year-old-link|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404114938/https://www.shacknews.com/article/99115/the-legend-of-zelda-art-book-reveals-a-25-30-year-old-link|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-19|website=Shacknews}}</ref> Aonuma explained that the design team wanted to express Link as an older teenager: "The fact that we're seeing Link on horseback and swinging a sword, I think that's one more way in which Link has matured".<ref name=":6" /> In ''Twilight Princess'', Link has the ability to transform into a wolf. Aonuma said that the design team considered the animal to be the best representation of a hero and that this function gave the adult version of Link limited human abilities, allowing him to mature over the course of the game.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Casamassina|first=Matt|date=2005-05-19|title=E3 2005: Eiji Aonuma Interview|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2005/05/19/e3-2005-eiji-aonuma-interview|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404183329/https://www.ign.com/articles/2005/05/19/e3-2005-eiji-aonuma-interview|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2022-03-06|website=IGN}}</ref>

[[File:Link of the Wild.png|thumb|alt=Artwork of Link dressed in a blue tunic and shooting an arrow|''[[The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild]]'' broke the conventions of Link's design by replacing his signature green hat and tunic.]]
Link's signature green tunic and cap have evolved over the course of the series. At a [[Game Developers Conference]] in San Francisco, Takizawa described the subtle changes made to Link's outfit. For ''Twilight Princess'' he, "made the hat long, so it would flap in the wind and move around", but for ''Skyward Sword'', he made it more diminutive and gave it less motion. The release of ''[[The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild|Breath of the Wild]]'' in 2017 broke the conventions of Link's design, notably by the absence of his signature green outfit. Takizawa explained that "as the graphic fidelity has increased it becomes more difficult to make that hat look cool".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Stark|first=Chelsea|date=2017-03-10|title=Why Link doesn't have his iconic, pointy hat in Zelda: Breath of the Wild|url=https://www.polygon.com/2017/3/10/14869766/zelda-breath-of-the-wild-link-hat|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230514000812/https://www.polygon.com/2017/3/10/14869766/zelda-breath-of-the-wild-link-hat|archive-date=14 May 2023|access-date=2021-10-08|website=Polygon}}</ref> For ''Breath of the Wild'' nearly 100 designs were considered to ensure that Link remained a neutral character. Aonuma commented: "We thought that the iconic green tunic and hat had become expected, so we wanted to mix things up and update his look. Interestingly, though, nobody on the team said, 'Let's make him blue!' It just organically ended up that way".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Craddock|first=Ryan|date=2018-12-17|title=Close To 100 Link Designs Were Considered For Zelda: Breath Of The Wild, Devs Explain Blue Tunic|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2018/12/close_to_100_link_designs_were_considered_for_zelda_breath_of_the_wild_devs_explain_blue_tunic|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404183301/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2018/12/close_to_100_link_designs_were_considered_for_zelda_breath_of_the_wild_devs_explain_blue_tunic|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-09|website=Nintendo Life}}</ref>

Link's physical features have also evolved, influenced by trends in masculinity. His handsomeness has been defined by soft, [[androgynous]] facial features, a sharp nose and a slight build. His hair has been inspired by the fashion of the era, beginning with a [[Mullet (haircut)|mullet]] in the 1980s, then followed by blond curtain [[Bangs (hair)|bangs]] and pierced ears in the 1990s, moving to choppy, layered side-swept bangs by the release of ''Twilight Princess''. His look was updated in ''Breath of the Wild'' to include a ponytail and a wide variety of outfits.<ref name=":10" /> Although Link is a male character, Aonuma said that he wanted him to be [[Gender neutrality|gender neutral]] in ''Ocarina of Time'': "I wanted the player to think 'Maybe Link is a boy or a girl'. If you saw Link as a guy, he'd have more of a feminine touch. Or vice versa, if you related to Link as a girl, it was with more of a masculine aspect". For ''Twilight Princess'', he created a more masculine Link, but later decided to return him to a more gender neutral character. He said that Link is definitely male in ''Breath of the Wild'', but wanted to create a character to which anyone could relate.<ref name="BotWLink">{{Cite magazine |last=Peckham |first=Matt |author-link=Matt Peckham |date=2016-06-15 |title=Next Link May Not Be a Girl, But He's Androgynous by Design |url=https://time.com/4369537/female-link-zelda/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230920234302/https://time.com/4369537/female-link-zelda/ |archive-date=20 September 2023 |access-date=2023-10-29 |magazine=Time}}</ref>

In multiple entries, Link has a counterpart character called either Shadow Link or Dark Link.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://kotaku.com/toy-time-plays-with-the-legend-of-zelda-shadow-link-col-1782477561 |title= The Shadow Link Collection Takes The Legend Of Zelda To A Dark Place |work=[[Kotaku]] |last=Fahey |first=Mike |date=June 25, 2016 |accessdate=August 24, 2025}}</ref> This version has different designs; in ''Zelda II'', he has a shadowy appearance,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.inverse.com/gaming/zelda-2-adventure-of-link-divisive-nintendo-sequel-retrospective |title=35 Years Ago, the Most Divisive Zelda Game Changed the Series Forever |work=[[Inverse (website)|Inverse]] |last=Bea |first=Robin |date=December 3, 2023 |accessdate=August 24, 2025}}</ref> while in ''Ocarina of Time'', he also has red eyes.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://gameinformer.com/blogs/editors/b/gijeffa_blog/archive/2011/09/26/dark-link-statue-is-impressive-and-dark.aspx |title=Dark Link Statue Is Impressive...And Dark |work=[[Game Informer]] |last=Akervik |first=Jeff |date=September 26, 2011 |accessdate=August 24, 2025}}</ref>

=== Portrayal ===
Link has been voiced by eight actors in the series and also voiced in other games: [[Nobuyuki Hiyama]] in ''Ocarina of Time'' as adult Link, ''[[Majora's Mask]]'' as Fierce Deity Link, ''[[Super Smash Bros. (video game)|Super Smash Bros.]]'', ''[[Super Smash Bros. Melee]]'' and ''[[Soulcalibur II]]'',<ref name="link voices">{{Cite web|title=Link Voices (Legend of Zelda)|url=https://www.behindthevoiceactors.com/characters/The-Legend-of-Zelda/Link/|access-date=March 23, 2024|website=Behind The Voice Actors|archive-date=April 4, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404100903/https://www.behindthevoiceactors.com/characters/The-Legend-of-Zelda/Link/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Fujiko Takimoto]] in ''Ocarina of Time'' as young Link, ''Majora's Mask'', ''[[The Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past and Four Swords|A Link to the Past and Four Swords]]'', and as Young Link in ''[[Hyrule Warriors]]'', ''Super Smash Bros. Melee'' and ''[[Super Smash Bros. Ultimate]]'',<ref name="link voices"/> [[Sachi Matsumoto]] in ''[[The Wind Waker]]'', ''[[Phantom hourglass|Phantom Hourglass]]'', ''[[Four Swords Adventures]]'' and as Toon Link in ''Hyrule Warriors'', ''[[Super Smash Bros. Brawl]]'' and ''Super Smash Bros. Ultimate'',<ref name="link voices"/> [[Akira Sasanuma]] in ''[[Twilight Princess]]'', ''Super Smash Bros. Brawl''<ref name="link voices"/> and ''[[Link's Crossbow Training]]'',<ref>{{Cite web|date=2006-12-02|title=Akira Sasanuma|url=https://www.metacritic.com/person/akira-sasanuma/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230918065509/https://www.metacritic.com/person/akira-sasanuma/|archive-date=18 September 2023|access-date=2023-09-15|website=Metacritic}}</ref> Yūki Kodaira in ''[[Spirit Tracks]]'',<ref name="link voices"/> [[Takashi Ōhara]] in ''[[Skyward Sword]]'',<ref name="link voices"/> [[Mitsuki Saiga]] in ''[[A Link Between Worlds]]'' and ''[[Link's Awakening (2019 video game)|Link's Awakening]]'' (2019),<ref name="link voices"/> and Kengo Takanashi in ''[[Breath of the Wild]]'', ''[[Hyrule Warriors: Age of Calamity]]'', ''Super Smash Bros. Ultimate'' and ''[[Tears of the Kingdom]]''.<ref name="link voices"/> Link was voiced by [[Yuki Kaji]] in ''Hyrule Warriors''.<ref name="link voices"/> In ''[[Cadence of Hyrule]]'', he was voiced by [[Caitlyn Bairstow]].<ref name="link voices"/> In the cutscenes of the CD-i titles [[Link: The Faces of Evil and Zelda: The Wand of Gamelon|''Link: The Faces of Evil'' and ''Zelda: The Wand of Gamelon'']], he was voiced by Jeffrey Rath.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Doolan|first=Liam|date=2023-07-13|title=Arzette Is A "Spiritual Successor" To The Infamous Zelda CD-i Games And It's Coming To Switch In 2023|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2023/07/arzette-is-a-spiritual-successor-to-the-infamous-zelda-cd-i-games-and-its-coming-to-switch-in-2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230714133321/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2023/07/arzette-is-a-spiritual-successor-to-the-infamous-zelda-cd-i-games-and-its-coming-to-switch-in-2023|archive-date=14 July 2023|access-date=2023-09-15|website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> On television, Link was voiced by Jonathan Potts in both [[The Legend of Zelda (animated series)|''The Legend of Zelda'' animated series]] and ''[[Captain N: The Game Master]]''.<ref name="link voices"/>

== Characteristics ==
Link is the "Hero of Hyrule", the archetypal hero and protagonist of ''The Legend of Zelda'' series. Whenever a great malevolence threatens the Kingdom of Hyrule, typically in the form of Ganon, Link is the counterpart who is destined to fight that evil and restore peace to the people of Hyrule. He begins each game as either a child or young adult who is often lazy and unprepared for battle, but develops his skills and experience over the course of the game as the player upgrades his weapons and health. His story varies from game to game, but typically involves a similar thread of solving dungeon puzzles, saving [[Princess Zelda]] and defeating Ganon or some other evil.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|last=Thorpe|first=Nick|date=2023-04-07|title=The anatomy of Link: What makes Zelda's protagonist one of the greatest of all time?|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/the-anatomy-of-link-what-makes-zeldas-protagonist-one-of-the-greatest-of-all-time/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230530172306/https://www.gamesradar.com/the-anatomy-of-link-what-makes-zeldas-protagonist-one-of-the-greatest-of-all-time/|archive-date=30 May 2023|access-date=2023-09-16|website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> He displays the characteristic traits of the [[Hylian]] race, being of humanoid form with [[Elves in fiction|elfin]] features, including pointed ears. Since the first ''The Legend of Zelda'' video game, he has been repeatedly depicted wearing his characteristic green cap and tunic and typically wields a sword and shield.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Lambie|first=Ryan|date=2011-11-21|title=The evolution of The Legend Of Zelda's visual style|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-evolution-of-the-legend-of-zeldas-visual-style/|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220607132806/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-evolution-of-the-legend-of-zeldas-visual-style/|archive-date=7 June 2022|access-date=2021-10-11|website=Den of Geek}}</ref> Link's age varies across the series, starting the first game at ten years old and its sequel at 16. In ''Ocarina of Time'', he is initially aged nine and is aged up to 16 after the game's time shift. In ''The Wind Waker'', Toon Link is 12, whereas in ''Twilight Princess'', ''Skyward Sword'' and ''Breath of the Wild'', Link is aged 16 or 17 years old.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Greenbaum|first=Aaron|date=2022-08-04|title=The Legend of Zelda: How Old Are Link and Zelda Supposed to Be?|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/legend-of-zelda-how-old-are-link-and-zelda-every-game-age/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601035833/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/legend-of-zelda-how-old-are-link-and-zelda-every-game-age/|archive-date=1 June 2023|access-date=2023-09-20|website=Den of Geek}}</ref>

In the mythology of the series, Link is the reincarnated soul of a hero, chosen by the goddess [[Hylia (The Legend of Zelda)|Hylia]] to protect the kingdom of Hyrule from Ganon and save Princess Zelda whenever the need arises.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Oddo|first=Marco Vito|date=2021-07-20|title='Skyward Sword' Ending Explained: And So 'The Legend of Zelda' Begins|url=https://collider.com/skyward-sword-ending-explained/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230521150017/https://collider.com/skyward-sword-ending-explained/|archive-date=21 May 2023|access-date=2023-09-26|website=Collider}}</ref> He is also the bearer of the Triforce of Courage, one of the three components that combine to form the [[Triforce]], a sacred artefact and symbol of power.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Greenbaum|first=Aaron|date=2022-09-29|title=The Legend of Zelda: Who Created the Triforce?|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-who-created-the-triforce/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230513192839/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-who-created-the-triforce/|archive-date=13 May 2023|access-date=2023-09-16|website=Den of Geek}}</ref> The Triforce of Courage symbolises the essence of Link, specifically the courage and curiosity of the player setting out on the adventure.<ref name=":12">{{Cite news|date=2023-05-10|title=What Zelda means to us|work=Eurogamer.net|url=https://www.eurogamer.net/what-zelda-means-to-us|access-date=2023-09-20|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230618075354/https://www.eurogamer.net/what-zelda-means-to-us|archive-date=18 June 2023}}</ref> In several ''Zelda'' games, Link's main objective is to recover the fragments of the Triforce in order to defeat Ganon.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Scullion|first=Chris|date=2021-02-21|title=Happy 35th Birthday Zelda, one of gaming's greatest series|url=https://www.videogameschronicle.com/features/opinion/happy-35th-birthday-zelda-one-of-gamings-greatest-series/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230904180333/https://www.videogameschronicle.com/features/opinion/happy-35th-birthday-zelda-one-of-gamings-greatest-series/|archive-date=4 September 2023|access-date=2023-09-16|website=VGC}}</ref>

Although Link can carry an arsenal of weapons, including bows, boomerangs and magic rods,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Drake|first=Audrey|date=2011-03-22|title=The Legend of Zelda's Best Weapons|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/03/22/the-legend-of-zeldas-best-weapons|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230805185852/https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/03/22/the-legend-of-zeldas-best-weapons|archive-date=5 August 2023|access-date=2023-09-16|website=IGN}}</ref> his signature weapon is the [[Master Sword]], a powerful [[magic sword]] with the ability to repel evil.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Greenbaum|first=Aaron|date=2022-05-25|title=The Legend of Zelda: Who Forged the Master Sword?|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-master-sword-forged-origins-history-creator/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230425163454/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-master-sword-forged-origins-history-creator/|archive-date=25 April 2023|access-date=2023-09-16|website=Den of Geek}}</ref> Throughout each game, Link is able to obtain various items during his adventures, which the player can then use in gameplay. Many of these objects possess magical properties that bestow specific abilities on Link, such as a magic cape that makes Link invisible when he wears it, or potions that replenish his health. Others have various practical purposes, such as the hookshot, which enables Link to pull items towards him, and bombs for detonating walls. Link has used various musical instruments on his travels, most notably, the Ocarina of Time, which when played is used for teleportation.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Saenz|first=Jason|title=All 185 'Legend Of Zelda' Items, Ranked By Awesomeness|url=https://www.mtv.com/news/2019040/legend-zelda-items-ranked/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404185431/https://www.mtv.com/news/bnksz4/legend-zelda-items-ranked|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-14|website=MTV News}}</ref> In ''Breath of the Wild'', Link's key tool is the Sheikah Slate, a handheld tablet featuring various runes that enable him to manipulate the game world.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Statt|first=Nick|date=2017-03-07|title=Zelda Sheikah Slate review: Link's new phablet has magic, but no Netflix|url=https://www.theverge.com/tldr/2017/3/7/14809872/sheikah-slate-review-the-legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230616011928/https://www.theverge.com/tldr/2017/3/7/14809872/sheikah-slate-review-the-legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild|archive-date=16 June 2023|access-date=2021-10-14|website=The Verge}}</ref> In ''Tears of the Kingdom'' Link uses his powers such as Ultrahand, Fuse, and Ascend by replacing his damaged arm with the right arm of Rauru, the first King of Hyrule.<ref name="gamespot watts">{{Cite web |last=Watts |first=Steve |date=11 May 2023 |title=The Legend Of Zelda: Tears Of The Kingdom Review |url=https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/the-legend-of-zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-review/1900-6418063/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231009090542/https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/the-legend-of-zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-review/1900-6418063/ |archive-date=9 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-29 |website=GameSpot}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Reynolds |first=Matthew |last2=Lee |first2=Julia |date=May 12, 2023 |title=How to unlock all abilities in Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom |url=https://www.polygon.com/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-guide/23660112/abilities-list-fuse-ascend-recall-ultrahand-final-missing-ability/ |website=[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]] |location= |publisher= |access-date=October 4, 2025 }}</ref>

=== Relationships ===
Link's relationships with the other two main characters have been a defining aspect of the series. Ganon, Zelda and Link embody the three pieces of the [[Triforce]], with Ganon representing Power, Zelda representing Wisdom and Link representing Courage. This trinity dictates the enduring relationships of the three characters who are repeatedly reincarnated across the series in an endless battle for good and evil.<ref name=":12" /> The nature of Zelda and Link's relationship has been the subject of speculation and remains ambiguous. A romantic relationship was particularly emphasised in an official "romance trailer" for ''Skyward Sword''. In an interview with ''[[Game Informer]]'', Aonuma said, "we wanted the player to feel like this is a person who's very important to me, who I need to find. We used that hint of a romance between the two to tug at the heartstrings".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Byrd|first=Matthew|date=2021-07-15|title=Is Skyward Sword the Only Link and Zelda Romance Story?|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-link-romance-story-history-explained/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230725000009/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-link-romance-story-history-explained/|archive-date=25 July 2023|access-date=2021-10-11|website=Den of Geek}}</ref>

== Appearances ==
{{See also|List of The Legend of Zelda media#Video games}}

=== ''The Legend of Zelda'' series ===
[[File:Legend of Zelda NES.PNG|thumb|Link's first appearance in 2D form in ''[[The Legend of Zelda (video game)|The Legend of Zelda]]'' on the [[Nintendo Entertainment System|NES]] (1986)|alt=An overhead view of a young boy in a green tunic battling creatures]]
Link made his debut on the [[Nintendo Entertainment System]] (NES) in ''[[The Legend of Zelda (video game)|The Legend of Zelda]]'' (1986) as an [[8-bit Era|8-bit]] [[2D computer graphics|2D]] character within a flat environment viewed from a [[top-down perspective]].<ref name=":1" /> He was depicted as a short, stubby character with brown hair wearing a green cap and tunic.<ref name="EvolutionRadar">{{Cite web|last=Agnello|first=Anthony John|date=2016-02-19|title=The Legend of Zelda 1986 - 2016: The Evolution of Link|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/legend-zelda-1986-2016-evolution-link/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230403225325/https://www.gamesradar.com/legend-zelda-1986-2016-evolution-link/|archive-date=3 April 2023|access-date=2023-04-05|website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> During the game, he meets an old man who offers him a sword and declares "[[It's dangerous to go alone!]]". Link must rescue Princess Zelda and the kingdom of Hyrule from the evil wizard Ganon, who has stolen the [[Triforce of Power]]. He explores 128 areas of Hyrule and nine dungeons, which contain special items that help him to defeat each dungeon boss; he must defeat them all before fighting Ganon.<ref name="35yearszelda">{{Cite web|last=Mejia|first=Ozzie|date=21 February 2021|title=It's Dangerous to Go Alone: 35 Years of The Legend of Zelda|url=https://www.shacknews.com/article/122832/its-dangerous-to-go-alone-35-years-of-the-legend-of-zelda|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602144749/https://www.shacknews.com/article/122832/its-dangerous-to-go-alone-35-years-of-the-legend-of-zelda|archive-date=2 June 2023|access-date=2021-10-14|website=Shacknews}}</ref>

Link's second outing, ''[[Zelda II: The Adventure of Link]]'' (1987), depicted him as a slightly taller 2D sprite wearing a similar green outfit. In the production art, his outfit was largely the same, but he wore brown trousers and longer hair. His shield was decorated with a cross, but this was removed in the games that followed.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web|last=Otero|first=Jose|date=2 May 2017|title=The Legend of Zelda: Link's Stunning Visual Evolution|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2014/09/10/the-legend-of-zelda-links-stunning-visual-evolution|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404233450/https://www.ign.com/articles/2014/09/10/the-legend-of-zelda-links-stunning-visual-evolution|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=18 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> The game switches between top-down exploration and [[Side-scrolling video game|side-scrolling]] action sequences.<ref>{{Cite web|date=14 May 2012|title=The Many Looks of Link|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/11/28/the-many-looks-of-link|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230531220714/https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/11/28/the-many-looks-of-link|archive-date=31 May 2023|access-date=14 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> Link's combat capability evolved with the ability to stab up and down with his sword while jumping. [[Experience point]]s gained by defeating enemies enable his health and attack power to be levelled up. A series of magic spells also grant him special abilities, such as extra defence.<ref name="EvolutionRadar" /> To complete the adventure, Link must defeat his [[doppelgänger]] [[Dark Link]] to awaken sleeping Zelda.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Sliva|first=Marty|date=2023-04-26|title=Zelda II: The Adventure of Link Is a Bold and Important Failure|url=https://www.escapistmagazine.com/zelda-ii-the-adventure-of-link-is-a-bold-and-important-failure/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230918065509/https://www.escapistmagazine.com/zelda-ii-the-adventure-of-link-is-a-bold-and-important-failure/|archive-date=18 September 2023|access-date=2023-09-17|website=The Escapist}}</ref>

''[[A Link to the Past]]'' (1991) recreated Link in a [[16-bit era|16-bit]] colour palette for the [[Super Nintendo Entertainment System]], with the unusual characteristic of pink hair.<ref name=":5" /> By contrast, the game manual depicts Link as a tall blond character. Link's sprite was designed to appear more expressive, by featuring an animated hat and a face that turns red when pulling objects.<ref name=":5" /> The game reverted to a top-down perspective and features items that upgrade Link's moves with extra abilities such as running and swimming.<ref name="EvolutionRadar" /> It introduced several recurring elements, including the hookshot and the [[Master Sword]]. Link's combat abilities were developed to be more complex, giving him the ability to move diagonally, swing his sword in an arc and perform a spin attack that enables multi-directional attacks.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Thorpe|first=Nick|date=2023-04-12|title=The making of The Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/the-making-of-the-legend-of-zelda-a-link-to-the-past/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230726155352/https://www.gamesradar.com/the-making-of-the-legend-of-zelda-a-link-to-the-past/|archive-date=26 July 2023|access-date=2023-04-17|website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> His heart containers, which featured in the first game, were broken into pieces, necessitating the player to locate them and upgrade his health in smaller increments.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hicks|first=Linden|date=2022-04-02|title=A Link To The Past's 30th Anniversary: 10 Ways It Influences The Legend Of Zelda Today|url=https://screenrant.com/a-link-to-the-past-30th-anniversary-ways-it-influences-legend-of-zelda-today/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230930003955/https://screenrant.com/a-link-to-the-past-30th-anniversary-ways-it-influences-legend-of-zelda-today/|archive-date=30 September 2023|access-date=2023-04-06|website=ScreenRant}}</ref>

The first handheld title in the series, ''[[Link's Awakening]]'' (1993) on the [[Game Boy]], introduced Link in [[monochrome]] until the DX port brought the game to the [[Game Boy Color]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Lane|first=Gavin|date=2023-06-06|title=Which Version Of Zelda: Link's Awakening Is Your Favourite?|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/features/talking-point-which-version-of-zelda-links-awakening-is-your-favourite|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230612191739/https://www.nintendolife.com/features/talking-point-which-version-of-zelda-links-awakening-is-your-favourite|archive-date=12 June 2023|access-date=2023-09-26|website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> It takes place on Koholint Island after Link is washed ashore and features a combination of mostly top-down adventuring and some side-scrolling action sequences. It was the first overhead game in which Link's move set includes the ability to jump. Link is depicted in an [[anime]] art style in the game's cutscenes.<ref name="EvolutionRadar" /> The game involves collecting eight musical instruments from eight dungeons to wake the sleeping Wind Fish.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Thomas|first=Lucas|date=6 May 2012|title=The Legend of Zelda: Link's Awakening DX Review|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/06/14/the-legend-of-zelda-links-awakening-dx-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230531110145/https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/06/14/the-legend-of-zelda-links-awakening-dx-review|archive-date=31 May 2023|access-date=18 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref>

[[File:ZELDA OCARINA OF TIME.jpg|thumb|alt=A screenshot of Link in 3D form standing in a field in Hyrule|''[[The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time]]'' was the first game in the series to render Link in 3D and extensively expanded on his range of movements.]]
''[[Ocarina of Time]]'' (1998), which was released for the [[Nintendo 64]] (N64), was the first [[3D computer graphics|3D]] game in the ''Zelda'' series. Link was depicted with blonde hair and his horse [[Epona (The Legend of Zelda)|Epona]] was introduced as a mode of transport.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web|last=Starr|first=Michelle|date=19 February 2016|title=30 years of Zelda: See the Hero of Time through the ages (pictures)|url=https://www.cnet.com/pictures/30-years-of-zelda-see-the-hero-of-time-through-the-ages-pictures/2/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224805/https://www.cnet.com/pictures/30-years-of-zelda-see-the-hero-of-time-through-the-ages-pictures/2/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-16|website=CNET}}</ref> The plot's time shifts allow the player to control Link first as a child and later as a young adult. His green outfit reflects his role in the story as a [[Universe of The Legend of Zelda|Kokiri]] and his shield was updated to display the crest of the kingdom of Hyrule.<ref name=":5" /> His abilities were extensively developed to perform a variety of movements, including a swipe, stab, roll, dodge and dive. The game's Z-targeting system enables him to lock on to enemies and circle around them in combat.<ref name="EvolutionRadar" /> Through a built-in autojump function, he can also jump from a ledge.<ref name="IGNOcarina">{{Cite web|last=Schneider|first=Peer|date=1998-11-26|title=The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time Review|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/1998/11/26/the-legend-of-zelda-ocarina-of-time-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231014225750/https://www.ign.com/articles/1998/11/26/the-legend-of-zelda-ocarina-of-time-review|archive-date=14 October 2023|access-date=2023-09-22|website=IGN}}</ref> During his adventure, he is accompanied by the fairy [[Navi (The Legend of Zelda)|Navi]], who gives him hints about enemies and his surroundings.<ref name="IGNOcarina" />

Link returned on the N64 with the release of ''[[Majora's Mask]]'' (2000). Appearing again as a child, he must save the land of Termina from the evil of Majora's Mask, which has drawn the moon into a decaying orbit, threatening to crash into Termina's Clock Town in three days. Link uses the Ocarina of Time to play the Song of Time, which allows him to repeatedly travel back in time to relive the three days until he successfully prevents the disaster. During the game, Link finds various magical masks that transform him into a [[Goron]], [[Deku Scrub]], or [[Zoras|Zora]], which each grant him special abilities such as gliding, swimming underwater and rolling.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Dring|first=Chris|date=2015-02-16|title=The Legend of Zelda: Majora's Mask review – definitive take on one of Nintendo's best|url=http://www.theguardian.com/technology/2015/feb/16/legend-zelda-majoras-mask-review-nintendo-3ds|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230509141452/https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2015/feb/16/legend-zelda-majoras-mask-review-nintendo-3ds|archive-date=9 May 2023|access-date=2021-10-17|website=the Guardian}}</ref> In the final boss battle with [[Majora (The Legend of Zelda)|Majora]], Link is transformed into Fierce Deity Link, a dark, adult version of himself with white eyes, before finally reverting to his child form.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Richtmyer|first=Steven|date=2021-08-21|title=Zelda: How Fierce Deity Represents Link's Lost Childhood|url=https://screenrant.com/zelda-majoras-mask-fierce-deity-link-lost-childhood/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224201/https://screenrant.com/zelda-majoras-mask-fierce-deity-link-lost-childhood/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-19|website=ScreenRant}}</ref>

In [[The Legend of Zelda: Oracle of Seasons and Oracle of Ages|''Oracle of Seasons'' and ''Oracle of Ages'']], a pair of interconnected games released in 2001 for the [[Game Boy Color]], Link is controlled from a top-down perspective within a flat 2D game world.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Lane|first=Gavin|date=2021-03-15|title=The Brilliance Of Zelda: Oracle Of Ages & Seasons Forced Nintendo To Up Its Game|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2021/03/feature_the_brilliance_of_zelda_oracle_of_ages_and_seasons_forced_nintendo_to_up_its_game|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404100903/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2021/03/feature_the_brilliance_of_zelda_oracle_of_ages_and_seasons_forced_nintendo_to_up_its_game|archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-10-17|website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> The two games involve Link being sent on a mission to the foreign lands of Holodrum and Labrynna in order to stop the disruption of the seasons by the General of Darkness, Onox, and the disruption of time by the Sorceress of Shadows, Veran. Link's key tools for uncovering areas and fighting his enemies are the Rod of Seasons and his magic harp.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bramwell|first=Tom|date=2001-10-17|title=The Legend of Zelda: Oracle of Ages, Oracle of Seasons|url=https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/r_zelda_gbc|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404190103/https://www.eurogamer.net/r-zelda-gbc|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-19|website=Eurogamer}}</ref>

In 2002, Nintendo published ''[[The Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past & Four Swords|A Link to the Past & Four Swords]]'' on the [[Game Boy Advance]]. ''Four Swords'' was an original title packaged together with the rereleased entry ''A Link to the Past''. Diverging from the traditional [[Single-player video game|single-player]] approach, ''Four Swords'' was based on [[Cooperative video game|cooperative gameplay]], allowing four players to interact together by connecting four Game Boy Advance systems.<ref>{{Cite magazine|last=Shea|first=Brian|date=23 May 2023|title=Ranking Every Legend Of Zelda Game|url=https://www.gameinformer.com/b/features/archive/2017/02/17/legend-of-zelda-ranking-every-game.aspx|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231004032042/https://www.gameinformer.com/b/features/archive/2017/02/17/legend-of-zelda-ranking-every-game.aspx|archive-date=4 October 2023|access-date=2023-10-29|url-status=live|magazine=Game Informer}}</ref> In ''Four Swords'', four Links of different colours appear, each of which is controlled by a different player. The four versions of Link must then work together to complete the game's challenges.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Harris|first=Craig|date=6 September 2002|title=Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2002/09/06/legend-of-zelda-a-link-to-the-past-2|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224201/https://www.ign.com/articles/2002/09/06/legend-of-zelda-a-link-to-the-past-2|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=19 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref>

''[[The Wind Waker]]'', which was released in 2002 on the [[GameCube]], depicted Link and the game world in a cute childlike graphical style. He appears as a preteen cartoon character with a large head and huge eyes.<ref name="EuroHD">{{Cite web|last=Welsh|first=Oli|date=2013-09-17|title=The Legend of Zelda: The Wind Waker HD review|url=https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/2013-09-17-the-legend-of-zelda-the-wind-waker-hd-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230724115231/https://www.eurogamer.net/the-legend-of-zelda-the-wind-waker-hd-review|archive-date=24 July 2023|access-date=2021-10-17|website=Eurogamer}}</ref> His facial expressions communicate a range of emotions.<ref name="EvolutionRadar" /> The game involves exploring the islands that are dotted around the map to encounter characters and uncover dungeons.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Gilbert|first=Henry|date=2013-09-18|title=The Legend of Zelda: The Wind Waker HD review|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/uk/legend-of-zelda-the-wind-waker-hd-review/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224208/https://www.gamesradar.com/legend-of-zelda-the-wind-waker-hd-review/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-18|website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> Link's key tool is the titular Wind Waker, a magical baton that enables him to manipulate the breeze as he crosses the Great Sea.<ref>{{Cite magazine|last=Shea|first=Brian|date=27 April 2016|title=Replica Of Wind Waker Baton From Zelda Available For Pre-Order|url=https://www.gameinformer.com/b/news/archive/2016/04/27/replica-of-wind-waker-baton-from-zelda-available-for-pre-order.aspx|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404190934/https://www.gameinformer.com/b/news/archive/2016/04/27/replica-of-wind-waker-baton-from-zelda-available-for-pre-order.aspx|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2023-10-29|url-status=live|magazine=Game Informer}}</ref> Nintendo artist Satoru Takizawa explained that the game's sequel was abandoned due to being set on land and Toon Link's proportions not being suitable for horseback riding.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Dayus|first=Oscar|date=6 December 2016|title=Zelda: Wind Waker 2 Was Cancelled By Nintendo|url=https://www.gamespot.com/articles/zelda-wind-waker-2-was-cancelled-by-nintendo/1100-6446007/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224201/https://www.gamespot.com/articles/zelda-wind-waker-2-was-cancelled-by-nintendo/1100-6446007/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-18|website=GameSpot}}</ref>

''[[Four Swords Adventures]]'', which was published for the GameCube in 2004, again reinterpreted traditional ''Zelda'' gameplay with a [[Multiplayer video game|multiplayer]] approach. The plot centres on Link being split into four versions of himself by the Four Sword, resulting in four Links of different colours. He must embark on a quest that involves rescuing seven princesses from an evil sorcerer. The game depicts the Links in a 16-bit top-down perspective and involves each player controlling one of the four Links to explore the game world and defeat enemies in order to complete each level.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bramwell|first=Tom|date=2005-01-25|title=The Legend of Zelda: Four Swords Adventures|url=https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/r_fourswordsadventures_gc|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224504/https://www.eurogamer.net/r-fourswordsadventures-gc|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-19|website=Eurogamer}}</ref>

The release of ''[[The Minish Cap]]'' in 2004 on the Game Boy Advance returned the series to a single-player format. Link appears as a child viewed from a top-down perspective and is tasked with saving his friend Princess Zelda. After a mysterious stranger arrives at a festival celebrating the coming of the [[Universe of The Legend of Zelda#Minish|Picori]], he destroys the sacred Picori Blade and turns Zelda to stone. During the game, Link is accompanied by a magical talking cap named Ezlo, who helps him on his quest and teaches him to shrink to the size of the Minish.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bramwell|first=Tom|date=2004-11-18|title=The Legend of Zelda: The Minish Cap|url=https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/r_zeldaminishcap_gba|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230902214245/https://www.eurogamer.net/r-zeldaminishcap-gba|archive-date=2 September 2023|access-date=2021-10-19|website=Eurogamer}}</ref>

''[[Twilight Princess]]'' (2006) was released on the GameCube and Wii and offers a darker adventure to previous entries in the series. The game features a more detailed depiction of a teenage Link who appears ready for battle wearing chainmail beneath his tunic. In the early part of the game, he becomes trapped in the Twilight, which transforms him into a wolf.<ref name=":5" /> Throughout the game, he is aided by [[Midna]], an imp-like creature, who rides on his back and helps him on his quest. The game uses a variety of animal abilities for progression, such as the ability to follow scents.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Casamassina|first=Matt|date=18 November 2006|title=The Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess Review|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2006/11/18/the-legend-of-zelda-twilight-princess-review-2|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230923202434/https://www.ign.com/articles/2006/11/18/the-legend-of-zelda-twilight-princess-review-2|archive-date=23 September 2023|access-date=19 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> ''Twilight Princess'' aimed to provide a stark contrast to the cartoonish style of ''The Wind Waker'', presenting a game world drained of colour and a story with a more mature tone.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Oddo|first=Marco Vito|date=2021-08-01|title=By Making a Mature 'Zelda' Game, 'Twilight Princess' Sucked the Fun Out of the Franchise|url=https://collider.com/twilight-princess-zelda-games-legacy/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224757/https://collider.com/twilight-princess-zelda-games-legacy/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-19|website=Collider}}</ref> In addition to the teenage protagonist Link, the game also features the Link from ''[[Ocarina of Time]]'' and ''[[Majora's Mask]]'' as the teenage Link's ancestor and namesake "the Hero's Shade", who trains his descendant to defeat Ganondorf.<ref>"Link, the descendant of the Hero of Time: [...] The spirit of Link's ancestor, the Hero of Time, teaches him his secrets. Ever since returning to the Child Era, the swordsman has lamented the fact that he was not remembered as a hero. This is the reason he passes down the proof of his courage and his secret techniques to the Link of this era, addressing him as "son"." ''[[Hyrule Historia]]'', [[Dark Horse Books]], pg. 118</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Fontes|first=Renan|date=2020-06-29|title=Zelda: Who Is The Hero's Shade & 9 Other Questions Hyrule Historia Answers|url=https://gamerant.com/zelda-questions-answered-by-hyrule-historia|access-date=2020-06-29|website=Game Rant|archive-date=2021-09-11|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210911074220/https://gamerant.com/zelda-questions-answered-by-hyrule-historia/|url-status=live}}</ref>

A direct sequel to ''The Wind Waker'', ''[[Phantom Hourglass]]'', was released in 2007 for the [[Nintendo DS]]. It follows Link as he embarks on a journey to save his friend Tetra. The game was designed to appeal to a wider audience, with 3D cel-shaded graphics and a light-hearted tone.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bozon|first=Mark|date=25 September 2007|title=The Legend of Zelda: Phantom Hourglass Review|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/09/25/the-legend-of-zelda-phantom-hourglass-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230623152954/https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/09/25/the-legend-of-zelda-phantom-hourglass-review|archive-date=23 June 2023|access-date=20 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> It takes inspiration from the style and tone of ''The Wind Waker'', with Toon Link displaying various humorous expressions.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Gray|first=Kate|date=2021-04-03|title=Zelda: Phantom Hourglass Is Secretly One Of The Best Zeldas|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2021/04/feature_zelda_phantom_hourglass_is_secretly_one_of_the_best_zeldas|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404114938/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2021/04/feature_zelda_phantom_hourglass_is_secretly_one_of_the_best_zeldas|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-20|website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> The game made use of the console's [[touchscreen]] functionality, which enabled the player to control Link using a [[Stylus (computing)|stylus]] instead of the traditional button controls.<ref>{{Cite web|last=West|first=Josh|date=2021-07-16|title=The ultimate guide to The Legend of Zelda games|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/ultimate-guide-to-the-legend-of-zelda-games/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404074632/https://www.gamesradar.com/ultimate-guide-to-the-legend-of-zelda-games/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-20 |website=GamesRadar+}}</ref>

Following on from ''Phantom Hourglass'', ''[[Spirit Tracks]]'' (2009) reintroduced Toon Link to the Nintendo DS. The game is a sequel to its predecessor and has a similar format but replaces sailing by boat with travelling by train. Link must explore four main map pieces, return the power of the spirit tracks using the Spirit Flute, and navigate the Spirit Tower, the main labyrinth hub, aided by the spirit of Zelda.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bozon|first=Mark|date=7 December 2009|title=The Legend of Zelda: Spirit Tracks Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2009/12/07/the-legend-of-zelda-spirit-tracks-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602123351/https://www.ign.com/articles/2009/12/07/the-legend-of-zelda-spirit-tracks-review|archive-date=2 June 2023|access-date=20 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> The gameplay also incorporates the mechanic of controlling both Link and Zelda in a series of cooperative puzzles and boss battles.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Ramsay|first=Randolf|date=8 December 2009|title=The Legend of Zelda: Spirit Tracks Review|url=https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/the-legend-of-zelda-spirit-tracks-review/1900-6242040/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230620132135/https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/the-legend-of-zelda-spirit-tracks-review/1900-6242040/|archive-date=20 June 2023|access-date=2021-10-20|website=GameSpot}}</ref>

''[[Skyward Sword]]'' was released in 2011 on the [[Wii]] and made use of the [[Wii MotionPlus]] to swipe Link's sword, control a mechanical beetle, and ride his loftwing through the skies.<ref>{{Cite web|last=George|first=Richard|date=11 November 2011|title=The Legend of Zelda: Skyward Sword Review|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/11/11/the-legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230629234715/https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/11/11/the-legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-review|archive-date=29 June 2023|access-date=18 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> The plot involves a romantic relationship between Link and Zelda. Beginning in Skyloft, a land floating above the clouds, Link is forced to descend to the surface after Zelda is kidnapped and then travels between the two lands wielding the Goddess Sword, a magical sword that holds a spirit named [[Fi (The Legend of Zelda)|Fi]], who acts as his guide. During his journey, Link imbues the Goddess Sword with three sacred flames and reforges it into the Master Sword.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Byrd|first=Matthew|date=2021-07-15|title=Why The Legend of Zelda: Skyward Sword Is an Awkward Origin Story|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-origin-story-explained-problems-plot-holes/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224802/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-origin-story-explained-problems-plot-holes/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-15|website=Den of Geek}}</ref> The game introduced Link's stamina meter for climbing surfaces and sprinting, and the ability to craft materials to make new items.<ref name="EvolutionRadar" />

''[[A Link Between Worlds]]'' was released in 2013 for the [[Nintendo 3DS]] and is a sequel to ''A Link to the Past''. The plot revolves around a sorcerer named Yuga who has the ability to merge into walls and turn people into paintings. Link must foil his plan to revive Ganon. The storyline is set within the same world as ''A Link to the Past'' and was designed with a similar art style. Players are able to explore the two separate worlds of Hyrule and Lorule, which are reminiscent of the contrasting worlds of light and dark in ''A Link to the Past''. The game introduced the ability for Link to turn into a painting, allowing him to walk along walls and switch between the two worlds.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Webster|first=Andrew|date=2013-11-14|title='The Legend of Zelda: A Link Between Worlds' reminds you why you loved Zelda|url=https://www.theverge.com/2013/11/14/5103870/legend-of-zelda-a-link-between-worlds-gets-back-to-series-roots|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602183706/https://www.theverge.com/2013/11/14/5103870/legend-of-zelda-a-link-between-worlds-gets-back-to-series-roots|archive-date=2 June 2023|access-date=2021-10-17|website=The Verge}}</ref>

''[[Tri Force Heroes]]'' (2015) was the second original ''Zelda'' entry for the Nintendo 3DS and a departure from the series' tradition, with a focus on a three-player [[Cooperative video game|cooperative]] campaign. Link appears alongside two companions that can be stacked to reach high places, solve puzzles and defeat tall enemies.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Otero|first=Jose|date=16 June 2016|title=E3 2015: The Legend of Zelda: Triforce Heroes Revealed for 3DS|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/06/16/e3-2015-the-legend-of-zelda-triforce-heroes-revealed-for-3ds|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610042056/https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/06/16/e3-2015-the-legend-of-zelda-triforce-heroes-revealed-for-3ds|archive-date=10 June 2023|access-date=18 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref> The storyline revolves around the theme of fashion and is set in the realm of Hytopia, where a witch has cursed Princess Styla to wear an ugly brown body suit. As a result, the Hytopian king calls for adventurers to break the curse. The gameplay departs from the usual ''Zelda'' open-world structure and involves completing dungeons and collecting materials to fashion outfits that provide specific abilities.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mahardy|first=Mike|date=22 October 2015|title=The Legend of Zelda: Tri Force Heroes Review|url=https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/the-legend-of-zelda-tri-force-heroes-review/1900-6416288/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230908225135/https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/the-legend-of-zelda-tri-force-heroes-review/1900-6416288/|archive-date=8 September 2023|access-date=2021-10-18|website=GameSpot}}</ref>

''[[Breath of the Wild]]'' was released on the [[Nintendo Switch]] and [[Wii U]] in 2017 and reinvented the franchise by creating a massive [[open world]] for Link to explore. He has the ability to climb most surfaces, jump from structures and soar across the sky using a paraglider. He was designed to interact with the environment, collect materials for cooking and react to cold and heat.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Webster|first=Andrew|date=2017-03-02|title=The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild review|url=https://www.theverge.com/2017/3/2/14787082/the-legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231021035118/https://www.theverge.com/2017/3/2/14787082/the-legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-review|archive-date=21 October 2023|access-date=2021-10-18|website=The Verge}}</ref> The complex nature of the environment also determined that his preexisting autojump functionality had to be developed into the ability to jump manually.<ref>{{Cite magazine|last=Peckham|first=Matt|date=2016-06-15|title=Why Nintendo Had to Destroy Tradition to Create the New 'Zelda'|url=https://time.com/4369527/zelda-breath-wild-open-world/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231009030546/https://time.com/4369527/zelda-breath-wild-open-world/|archive-date=9 October 2023|access-date=2023-10-29|magazine=Time}}</ref> The game introduced significant changes to his design, notably the absence of his signature green outfit by default. The player can instead dress Link in a variety of outfits and more emphasis is placed on using a bow as a prominent weapon.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Barder|first=Ollie|title=There Is A Reason Why Link Doesn't Have His Iconic Green Tunic In 'Zelda: Breath Of The Wild'|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/olliebarder/2016/10/03/there-is-a-reason-why-link-doesnt-have-his-iconic-green-tunic-in-zelda-breath-of-the-wild/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224201/https://www.forbes.com/sites/olliebarder/2016/10/03/there-is-a-reason-why-link-doesnt-have-his-iconic-green-tunic-in-zelda-breath-of-the-wild/|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-15|website=Forbes}}</ref>

Link appears in ''[[The Legend of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom|Tears of the Kingdom]]'',<ref>{{cite news|last=Robinson|first=Andy|date=September 13, 2022|title=Zelda: BoTW's sequel is officially releasing in May 2023 as 'Tears of the Kingdom'|work=[[Video Games Chronicle]]|url=https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/zelda-botws-sequel-is-officially-releasing-in-may-2023-as-tears-of-the-kingdom/|access-date=September 14, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230513231708/https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/zelda-botws-sequel-is-officially-releasing-in-may-2023-as-tears-of-the-kingdom/|archive-date=13 May 2023}}</ref> a direct sequel to the previous game released in 2023, which is set in the same version of Hyrule.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Diaz|first=Ana|date=2021-06-15|title=Breath of the Wild 2 upgrades Link's hair to glorious|url=https://www.polygon.com/e3/22535294/legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-2-link-nintendo-switch|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404074636/https://www.polygon.com/e3/22535294/legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-2-link-nintendo-switch|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2021-10-15|website=Polygon}}</ref> Link loses his right arm after being attacked by a substance called Gloom and is saved by the spirit Rauru who grafts his own arm onto Link.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Greenbaum|first=Aaron|date=2023-05-15|title=Tears of the Kingdom: How to Unlock Every Main Ability|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/tears-of-the-kingdom-unlock-every-main-ability/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230617215749/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/tears-of-the-kingdom-unlock-every-main-ability/|archive-date=17 June 2023|access-date=2023-06-17|website=Den of Geek}}</ref> This new arm plays a major role as the source of his abilities.<ref name=":11">{{Cite web|last=Bailey|first=Dustin|date=2023-02-09|title=Link's dead arm is still the wildest part of the Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom trailers|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/links-dead-arm-is-still-the-wildest-part-of-the-zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-trailers/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230425142501/https://www.gamesradar.com/links-dead-arm-is-still-the-wildest-part-of-the-zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-trailers/|archive-date=25 April 2023|access-date=2023-04-21|website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> It was designed to distinguish him from previous iterations, as the theme of "hands" was chosen as a key recurring aspect of the game for creating items as well as cooperating with other characters.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Valentine|first=Rebekah|date=2023-05-09|title=Zelda Developers Reveal Key Theme of Tears of the Kingdom: Hands|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/zelda-developers-reveal-key-theme-of-tears-of-the-kingdom-hands|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230522064235/https://www.ign.com/articles/zelda-developers-reveal-key-theme-of-tears-of-the-kingdom-hands|archive-date=22 May 2023|access-date=2023-05-13|website=IGN}}</ref> Link's abilities include the Recall ability, which rewinds an object's movement, the Fuse ability, which merges objects to create new weapons, the Ultrahand ability, which enables the construction of vehicles and structures, and the Ascend ability, which enables Link to rise through ceilings.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Plant|first=Logan|date=2023-03-28|title=Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom Gameplay Reveals Link's New Abilities, Weapon Durability, and More|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-gameplay-reveals-links-new-abilities-weapon-durability-and-more|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230604003520/https://www.ign.com/articles/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-gameplay-reveals-links-new-abilities-weapon-durability-and-more|archive-date=4 June 2023|access-date=2023-03-28|website=IGN}}</ref> In the main quest line, Link also obtains Autobuild, which gives the ability to rebuild vehicles automatically without needing to manually build with Ultrahand.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Reynolds|first=Matthew|date=2023-05-12|title=How to unlock Autobuild in Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom|url=https://www.polygon.com/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-guide/23719499/autobuild-unlock-get-great-abandoned-central-mine-location|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230806020536/https://www.polygon.com/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-guide/23719499/autobuild-unlock-get-great-abandoned-central-mine-location|archive-date=6 August 2023|access-date=2023-06-04|website=Polygon}}</ref>

''[[Echoes of Wisdom]]'' (2024) is the first mainline ''Zelda'' game to feature Zelda rather than Link as the player character.<ref>{{Cite web|last=McWhertor|first=Michael|date=2024-06-18|title=New Legend of Zelda game finally makes Zelda the playable hero of Hyrule|url=https://www.polygon.com/24180883/legend-of-zelda-echoes-wisdom-nintendo-switch-release-date|access-date=2024-06-18|website=Polygon|language=en-US|archive-date=2024-06-25|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240625000857/https://www.polygon.com/24180883/legend-of-zelda-echoes-wisdom-nintendo-switch-release-date|url-status=live}}</ref> Despite being originally intended as the protagonist, Link was ultimately replaced by Zelda as Aonuma felt that his sword fighting skills were not compatible with the copy and paste mechanism at the core of the game's echoes concept.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Martens|first=Todd|date=2024-10-01|title=Princess Zelda is the year's most butt-kicking childless cat lady|url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/story/2024-10-01/legend-zelda-echoes-wisdom-nintendo-video-game-review|access-date=2024-12-21|website=Los Angeles Times|language=en-US|archive-date=2024-12-09|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241209032412/https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/story/2024-10-01/legend-zelda-echoes-wisdom-nintendo-video-game-review|url-status=live}}</ref> The game begins with Link being playable in a sequence in which he saves Zelda from Ganon but is sucked through a portal into another dimension.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2024-06-18|title=The Legend of Zelda: Echoes of Wisdom will finally let you play as Zelda herself|url=https://www.engadget.com/the-legend-of-zelda-echoes-of-wisdom-will-finally-let-you-play-as-zelda-herself-143612318.html|access-date=2024-06-18|website=Engadget|language=en-US|archive-date=2024-07-08|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240708022310/https://www.engadget.com/the-legend-of-zelda-echoes-of-wisdom-will-finally-let-you-play-as-zelda-herself-143612318.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Norman |first=Jim |date=2024-10-04 |title=Random: Zelda: Echoes Of Wisdom Glitch Lets You Play As Link For Even Longer |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2024/10/random-zelda-echoes-of-wisdom-glitch-lets-you-play-as-link-for-even-longer |access-date=2025-01-21 |website=Nintendo Life |language=en-GB |archive-date=2025-03-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250313235649/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2024/10/random-zelda-echoes-of-wisdom-glitch-lets-you-play-as-link-for-even-longer |url-status=live }}</ref> Link makes numerous appearances throughout the game, both in real form and as an Echo mini boss. He also accompanies Zelda in the final dungeon and boss fight.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hagues|first=Alana|date=2024-10-10|title=Zelda: Echoes Of Wisdom: Is Link Playable?|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/guides/zelda-echoes-of-wisdom-is-link-playable#:~:text=Do%20You%20Play%20As%20Link,Of%20Zelda:%20Echoes%20Of%20Wisdom?&text=Yes,%20you%20do%20play%20as,the%20rest%20of%20the%20game.|access-date=2024-12-21|website=Nintendo Life|language=en-GB|archive-date=2024-10-14|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241014123602/https://www.nintendolife.com/guides/zelda-echoes-of-wisdom-is-link-playable#:~:text=Do%20You%20Play%20As%20Link,Of%20Zelda:%20Echoes%20Of%20Wisdom?&text=Yes,%20you%20do%20play%20as,the%20rest%20of%20the%20game.|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Spin-off games ===
In 1989, Link appeared in ''Zelda'', which was released in the [[Game & Watch]] series of [[handheld electronic game]]s. The unit features a multi-screen [[liquid-crystal display]] (LCD). The lower display renders Link fighting against dungeon monsters, while the upper display features the inventory.<ref>{{Cite web|last=McFerran|first=Damien|date=2021-06-16|title=Retro: Remember The First Time Nintendo Did A Zelda Game & Watch?|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2021/06/retro_remember_the_first_time_nintendo_did_a_zelda_game_and_watch|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224211/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2021/06/retro_remember_the_first_time_nintendo_did_a_zelda_game_and_watch|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2022-01-09|website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> The gameplay is simpler than the NES game and replaces Ganon with eight dragons that kidnap Zelda. After battling through a dungeon, Link must defeat a dragon on the upper screen to receive a piece of the Triforce, before freeing Zelda with the eight recovered Triforce pieces.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2009-12-22|title=Hyrule Times - Retrospective: Zelda Game & Watch|url=http://blogs.ign.com/Hyrule-Times/2006/06/17/21987|access-date=2022-01-09|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091222000158/http://blogs.ign.com/Hyrule-Times/2006/06/17/21987|archive-date=2009-12-22|website=IGN}}</ref> In the same year, ''The Legend of Zelda'' Game Watch was released, which was part of the LCD wristwatch product line licensed to and developed by [[Nelsonic Industries]]. In addition to its timekeeping function, the unit features gameplay based on the original ''The Legend of Zelda'' game but without a storyline. It features Link adventuring through eight dungeons comprising four rooms in each.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Eisenbeis|first=Richard|date=22 August 2014|title=A Brief History of Non-Nintendo Made Zelda Games|url=https://kotaku.com/a-brief-history-of-non-nintendo-made-zelda-games-1625435819|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404183257/https://kotaku.com/a-brief-history-of-non-nintendo-made-zelda-games-1625435819|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2022-01-09|website=Kotaku}}</ref>

He also appears in ''Zelda no Densetsu: Kamigami no Triforce'' (Barcode Battler II), which was released by [[Epoch Co.]] in 1992 in Japan but not in North America. It was developed for the [[Barcode Battler II]] console and involves swiping cards to unlock various characters from ''A Link to the Past''.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Kyle|first=Hilliard|title=Big Book of Zelda: The Unofficial Guide to Breath of the Wild and the Legend of Zelda|publisher=Triumph Books|year=2017}}</ref>

Link appears in [[Link: The Faces of Evil and Zelda: The Wand of Gamelon|''Link: The Faces of Evil'', ''Zelda: The Wand of Gamelon'']] and ''[[Zelda's Adventure]]'', [[The Legend of Zelda CD-i games|''The Legend of Zelda'' CD-i games]] published by [[Philips|Philips Interactive Media]] for its [[CD-i|Compact Disc-Interactive]] (CD-i) players. While the games were licensed by Nintendo to use official ''Zelda'' characters, they were not produced or supervised by Nintendo.<ref name="HT11">{{Cite web|last=Schneider|first=Peer|date=2001-12-07|title=Hyrule Times Vol. 11: Link: The Faces of Evil|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2001/12/07/hyrule-times-vol-11-link-the-faces-of-evil|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404074643/https://www.ign.com/articles/2001/12/07/hyrule-times-vol-11-link-the-faces-of-evil|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=2023-04-01|website=IGN}}</ref> As well as being critically panned, none of these games are recognised by Nintendo as part of the series' official chronology.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hagues|first=Alana|date=2023-04-01|title=Rumour: The Infamous Phillips CD-i Zelda Games Are Being Remastered For Switch|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2023/04/rumour-the-infamous-phillips-cd-i-zelda-games-are-being-remastered-for-switch|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230925032606/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2023/04/rumour-the-infamous-phillips-cd-i-zelda-games-are-being-remastered-for-switch|archive-date=25 September 2023|access-date=2023-04-01|website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> Unlike the main series, Link has the ability to speak. His personality mirrors his animated series counterpart in which he yearns for adventure and repeatedly attempts to get a kiss from Zelda.<ref name="HT11" />

Nintendo released a [[Shooting game|shooting video game]] in 2007 for the [[Wii]] titled ''[[Link's Crossbow Training]]''. It is set within the ''Zelda'' universe and features Link as a playable character holding a crossbow. The game revisits the world of ''Twilight Princess'' and features the same landmarks and enemies. It involves the use of the [[Wii Remote|Wii remote]] and nunchuck to create a light gun, which targets enemies with an onscreen reticule.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Casamassina|first=Matt|date=14 May 2012|title=Link's Crossbow Training Review|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/11/20/links-crossbow-training-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404191522/https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/11/20/links-crossbow-training-review|archive-date=4 April 2023|access-date=17 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref>

In the 2014 [[hack and slash]] video game ''[[Hyrule Warriors]]'' another incarnation appears as a Hyrulean soldier-in-training who helps to lead the campaign against the forces of evil.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Parkin|first=Simon|date=2014-09-17|title=Hyrule Warriors review|work=Eurogamer.net|url=https://www.eurogamer.net/hyrule-warriors-review|access-date=2023-09-22|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029161606/https://www.eurogamer.net/hyrule-warriors-review|archive-date=29 October 2023}}</ref> Young Link from ''Ocarina of Time'' and ''Majora's Mask'' also appears in the game as a playable character via [[downloadable content]],<ref>{{Cite web|last=Sadhev|first=Ishaan|date=2015-01-14|title=Tingle And Young Link Coming To Hyrule Warriors|url=https://www.siliconera.com/tingle-young-link-coming-hyrule-warriors/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132941/https://www.siliconera.com/tingle-young-link-coming-hyrule-warriors/|archive-date=29 October 2023|access-date=2023-09-22|website=Siliconera}}</ref> while Toon Link from ''The Wind Waker'' appears as a playable character in ''Hyrule Warriors Legends'', the Nintendo 3DS port of ''Warriors''.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hillier|first=Brenna|date=2015-11-19|title=Here's Toon Link in action in Hyrule Warriors Legends|url=https://www.vg247.com/heres-toon-link-in-action-in-hyrule-warriors-legends|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132941/https://www.vg247.com/heres-toon-link-in-action-in-hyrule-warriors-legends|archive-date=29 October 2023|access-date=2023-09-22|website=VG247}}</ref>

Link is also a playable character in the 2019 [[Nintendo Switch]] [[rhythm game]] ''[[Cadence of Hyrule]]'', a crossover between ''[[Crypt of the NecroDancer]]'' and ''The Legend of Zelda'' series.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Webster|first=Andrew|date=March 20, 2019|title=Cadence of Hyrule is an indie-developed Zelda spinoff for the Switch|url=https://www.theverge.com/2019/3/20/18273199/cadence-of-hyrule-zelda-spinoff-nintendo-switch|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230606024904/https://www.theverge.com/2019/3/20/18273199/cadence-of-hyrule-zelda-spinoff-nintendo-switch|archive-date=6 June 2023|website=The Verge}}</ref> Players can choose to play as either Link or Zelda and both are required at the end of the game. Link can perform a spin attack and carries a shield, while Zelda uses an ethereal block and a fireball.<ref>{{Cite web|last=James|first=Ford|date=2019-06-14|title=Cadence of Hyrule Link or Zelda: Which hero should you pick at the start of the game?|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/cadence-of-hyrule-link-or-zelda/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230509090743/https://www.gamesradar.com/cadence-of-hyrule-link-or-zelda/|archive-date=9 May 2023|access-date=2023-04-17|website=GamesRadar+}}</ref>

In ''[[Hyrule Warriors: Age of Calamity]]'', which acts as a prequel to ''Breath of the Wild'' and exists within the same game world, Link is accompanied by a roster of characters to fight alongside on the battlefield. The game progresses in the form of various missions that level up Link and his allies.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Shea|first=Cam|date=19 November 2020|title=Hyrule Warriors: Age of Calamity Review|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/hyrule-warriors-age-of-calamity-review|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230604042443/https://www.ign.com/articles/hyrule-warriors-age-of-calamity-review|archive-date=4 June 2023|access-date=14 October 2021|website=IGN}}</ref>

=== Other game series ===
Among [[Super Nintendo Entertainment System|SNES]] games, Link makes a cameo in ''[[Donkey Kong Country 2: Diddy's Kong Quest]]'''s "[[Cranky Kong|Cranky]]'s Video Game Heroes" where he is ranked against the player next to Mario and [[Yoshi]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hester |first=Larry |date=28 May 2013 |title=25 Things You Never Knew About "Donkey Kong" |url=https://www.complex.com/pop-culture/2013/05/25-things-you-never-knew-about-donkey-kong/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404100903/https://www.complex.com/pop-culture/2013/05/25-things-you-never-knew-about-donkey-kong |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-02-10 |website=Complex}}</ref> Alongside [[Samus Aran]], he makes a cameo in ''[[Super Mario RPG]]'', where they are seen sleeping in separate beds at an inn.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Hilliard |first=Kyle |title=Moments: Samus Aran And Link Appearing In Super Mario RPG |url=https://www.gameinformer.com/b/features/archive/2012/06/22/moments-samus-aran-and-link-appearing-in-super-mario-rpg.aspx |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404225410/https://www.gameinformer.com/b/features/archive/2012/06/22/moments-samus-aran-and-link-appearing-in-super-mario-rpg.aspx |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2023-10-30 |url-status=live |magazine=Game Informer}}</ref> Link can be also be found at the start of a challenge course in ''[[F1 Race]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Van Allen |first=Eric |date=2023-05-12 |title=An ode to Link's cameos in other game worlds |url=https://www.destructoid.com/recounting-links-cameos-in-other-games-legend-of-zelda-list/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132939/https://www.destructoid.com/recounting-links-cameos-in-other-games-legend-of-zelda-list/ |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-21 |website=Destructoid}}</ref>

He is a playable character in the ''[[Super Smash Bros.]]'' series and has appeared in every title since the first ''[[Super Smash Bros. (video game)|Super Smash Bros.]]'' on the [[Nintendo 64]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Smith |first=Donnie |date=2021-08-23 |title=Super Smash Bros Ultimate: Where Each Of Link's Alt Costumes Come From |url=https://screenrant.com/super-smash-bros-ultimate-where-each-of-links-alt-costumes-come-from/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404100923/https://screenrant.com/super-smash-bros-ultimate-where-each-of-links-alt-costumes-come-from/ |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-02-08 |website=ScreenRant}}</ref> Toon Link from ''The Wind Waker'' appeared in ''[[Super Smash Bros. Brawl]]'' on the Wii in 2008 and in [[Super Smash Bros. for Nintendo 3DS and Wii U|''Super Smash Bros. for Nintendo 3DS'' and ''Wii U'']] in 2014.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tach |first=Dave |date=2013-09-26 |title=Wind Waker's 'Toon Link' headed to Super Smash Bros. |url=https://www.polygon.com/2013/9/26/4773278/wind-waker-toon-link-super-smash-bros |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610102852/https://www.polygon.com/2013/9/26/4773278/wind-waker-toon-link-super-smash-bros |archive-date=10 June 2023 |access-date=2021-10-15 |website=Polygon}}</ref> Link later returned in ''[[Super Smash Bros. Ultimate]]'' for the Nintendo Switch in various incarnations, including green tunic Link, blue tunic Link from ''Breath of the Wild'', Toon Link and Young Link.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Green |first=Jake |date=2020-01-17 |title=Link Super Smash Bros Ultimate Guide - Unlock, Moves, Changes, Link Alternate Costumes, Final Smash |url=https://www.vg247.com/super-smash-bros-ultimate-character-every-fighter-in-smash-bros-ultimate?page=4 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029181801/https://www.vg247.com/super-smash-bros-ultimate-character-every-fighter-in-smash-bros-ultimate?page=4 |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-21 |website=VG247}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Green |first=Jake |date=2020-01-17 |title=Toon Link Super Smash Bros Ultimate Guide - Unlock, Moves, Changes, Toon Link Alternate Costumes, Final Smash |url=https://www.vg247.com/super-smash-bros-ultimate-character-every-fighter-in-smash-bros-ultimate?page=54 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230906153907/https://www.vg247.com/super-smash-bros-ultimate-character-every-fighter-in-smash-bros-ultimate?page=54 |archive-date=6 September 2023 |access-date=2023-09-21 |website=VG247}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Green |first=Jake |date=2020-01-17 |title=Young Link Super Smash Bros Ultimate Guide - Unlock, Moves, Changes, Young Link Alternate Costumes, Final Smash |url=https://www.vg247.com/super-smash-bros-ultimate-character-every-fighter-in-smash-bros-ultimate?page=28 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230906153909/https://www.vg247.com/super-smash-bros-ultimate-character-every-fighter-in-smash-bros-ultimate?page=28 |archive-date=6 September 2023 |access-date=2023-09-21 |website=VG247}}</ref>

In the GameCube version of [[Namco]]'s ''[[Soulcalibur II]]'', Link is a guest character.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hagues |first=Alana |date=2023-03-27 |title=Anniversary: Soulcalibur II Is 20 Years Old, And So Is Nintendo's Best Cameo |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2023/03/anniversary-soulcalibur-ii-is-20-years-old-and-so-is-nintendos-best-cameo |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132940/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2023/03/anniversary-soulcalibur-ii-is-20-years-old-and-so-is-nintendos-best-cameo |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-20 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> Miyamoto did not see a problem with Link appearing in a violent game, as he had already been established as a fighter in the ''Super Smash Bros.'' series.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2003-02-26 |title=Shigeru Miyamoto and Eiji Aonuma on Zelda: The Wind Waker  |work=Eurogamer.net |url=https://www.eurogamer.net/i-zelda-gc |access-date=2023-09-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404230009/https://www.eurogamer.net/i-zelda-gc |archive-date=4 April 2023}}</ref> As part of the styling of a ''Zelda''-themed game mode, he is featured in the 2006 puzzle video game ''[[Tetris DS]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bramwell |first=Tom |date=2006-04-04 |title=Tetris DS |url=https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/ss_tetris_ds |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404185436/https://www.eurogamer.net/ss-tetris-ds |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-02-14 |website=Eurogamer}}</ref> In ''[[Scribblenauts Unlimited]]'', many ''Zelda'' series characters are summonable in the Wii U version, including Link.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Phillips |first=Tom |date=2012-10-18 |title=Mario, Link and Zelda are in Scribblenauts Unlimited for Wii U |url=https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/2012-10-18-mario-link-and-zelda-are-in-scribblenauts-unlimited-for-wii-u |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404190107/https://www.eurogamer.net/mario-link-and-zelda-are-in-scribblenauts-unlimited-for-wii-u |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-10-15 |website=Eurogamer}}</ref> He is referenced by an archer dressed in green in ''[[The Legend of Zelda: Battle Quest]]'', a mini game in the 2012 party game ''[[Nintendo Land]]'', which involves archery and sword fighting in a cloth style.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Newton |first=James |date=2012-06-05 |title=E3 2012: Nintendo Land's Zelda Game Has Us in Stitches |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2012/06/e3_2012_nintendo_lands_zelda_game_has_us_in_stitches |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404183257/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2012/06/e3_2012_nintendo_lands_zelda_game_has_us_in_stitches |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-01-27 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> His costume appears alongside that of Samus in ''[[Dynasty Warriors|Dynasty Warriors VS]]'' for the Nintendo 3DS.<ref>{{Cite web |author1=Gilbert |first=Henry |date=2012-03-14 |title=Dynasty Warriors VS on 3DS will include Link and Samus costumes |url=https://www.gamesradar.com/uk/dynasty-warriors-vs-3ds-will-include-link-and-samus-costumes/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210414024317/https://www.gamesradar.com/uk/dynasty-warriors-vs-3ds-will-include-link-and-samus-costumes/ |archive-date=14 April 2021 |access-date=2022-02-05 |website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> Link appears in ''[[Sonic Lost World]]'' as part of "The Legend of Zelda Zone", where he rides his signature Crimson Loftwing and Sonic wears his green cap and tunic.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Otero |first=Jose |date=2014-03-26 |title=Sonic Lost World's Zelda Crossover |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2014/03/26/sonic-lost-worlds-zelda-crossover |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230821165133/https://www.ign.com/articles/2014/03/26/sonic-lost-worlds-zelda-crossover |archive-date=21 August 2023 |access-date=2023-09-22 |website=IGN}}</ref> In the 2013 compilation game ''[[NES Remix]]'', players can play remixed portions of NES games, including playing as Link in a level of ''[[Donkey Kong (arcade game)|Donkey Kong]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sahdev |first=Ishaan |date=2013-12-18 |title=Is That Link In Donkey Kong? Yes, It Is... In NES Remix For Wii U |url=https://www.siliconera.com/link-donkey-kong-yes-nes-remix-wii-u/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404100903/https://www.siliconera.com/link-donkey-kong-yes-nes-remix-wii-u/ |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-02-15 |website=Siliconera}}</ref>

The ''Skyward Sword'' incarnation of Link was a playable character in ''[[Mario Kart 8]]'' riding a motorcycle.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McWhertor |first=Michael |date=2014-11-13 |title=Link is now playable in Mario Kart 8 and is already responsible for the best Vine |url=https://www.polygon.com/2014/11/13/7216323/link-death-stare-mario-kart-8-vine |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230530180626/https://www.polygon.com/2014/11/13/7216323/link-death-stare-mario-kart-8-vine |archive-date=30 May 2023 |access-date=2021-10-14 |website=Polygon}}</ref> In ''[[Mario Kart 8 Deluxe]]'', his ''Breath of the Wild'' incarnation also appears.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McWhertor |first=Michael |date=2018-07-19 |title=Mario Kart 8 Deluxe update adds Link from Breath of the Wild |url=https://www.polygon.com/2018/7/19/17593958/mario-kart-8-deluxe-update-link-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-nintendo-switch |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221207192120/https://www.polygon.com/2018/7/19/17593958/mario-kart-8-deluxe-update-link-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-nintendo-switch |archive-date=7 December 2022 |access-date=2021-10-14 |website=Polygon}}</ref> He is referenced in 2015 downloadable content for ''[[Monster Hunter 4]]'' with armour resembling his tunic, ''[[Monster Hunter Generations]]'' DLC and also ''[[Monster Hunter Stories]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-03-01 |title=Monster Hunter 4 Ultimate Free DLC, Including Link Equipment, Due 6th March |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/03/monster_hunter_4_ultimate_free_dlc_including_link_equipment_due_6th_march |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150303130828/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/03/monster_hunter_4_ultimate_free_dlc_including_link_equipment_due_6th_march |archive-date=3 March 2015 |access-date=2021-10-15 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pereira |first=Chris |date=1 September 2016 |title=You Can Dress Up as Link and Ride Epona in Monster Hunter Stories |url=https://www.gamespot.com/articles/you-can-dress-up-as-link-and-ride-epona-in-monster/1100-6443234/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230816202753/https://www.gamespot.com/articles/you-can-dress-up-as-link-and-ride-epona-in-monster/1100-6443234/ |archive-date=16 August 2023 |access-date=2022-04-13 |website=GameSpot}}</ref> Incarnations of Link from ''The Legend of Zelda'', ''The Wind Waker'', ''Twilight Princess'' and ''Tri Force Heroes'' also appear as "Mystery Mushroom" costumes in ''[[Super Mario Maker]]''.<ref>{{cite web|date=19 August 2015|title=Super Mario Maker can also be Super Kirby, Zelda, Donkey Kong, etc. Maker|url=http://www.polygon.com/2015/8/19/9179669/super-mario-maker-costumes-amiibo-kirby|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160617200237/http://www.polygon.com/2015/8/19/9179669/super-mario-maker-costumes-amiibo-kirby|archive-date=2016-06-17|website=[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Carpenter |first=Nicole |date=2 May 2017 |title=Wolf Link Costume Coming to Super Mario Maker |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2016/03/02/wolf-link-costume-coming-to-super-mario-maker |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404160433/https://www.ign.com/articles/2016/03/02/wolf-link-costume-coming-to-super-mario-maker |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=15 October 2021 |website=IGN}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-11-05 |title=A Little Bit of The Legend of Zelda: Tri Force Heroes is Coming to Super Mario Maker |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/11/a_little_bit_of_the_legend_of_zelda_tri_force_heroes_is_coming_to_super_mario_maker |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224204/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/11/a_little_bit_of_the_legend_of_zelda_tri_force_heroes_is_coming_to_super_mario_maker |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-10-15 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> In 2016, Link appeared in a 3DS game titled ''My Nintendo Picross - The Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess'', a picture-puzzle game made available through the [[My Nintendo]] rewards program.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Brown |first=Mark |date=31 March 2016 |title=How to get a free Zelda Picross game for 3DS using My Nintendo and Miitomo |url=https://www.pocketgamer.com/miitomo/how-to-get-a-free-zelda-picross-game-for-3ds-using-my-nintendo-and-miitomo/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404185431/https://www.pocketgamer.com/miitomo/how-to-get-a-free-zelda-picross-game-for-3ds-using-my-nintendo-and-miitomo/ |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-01-09 |website=www.pocketgamer.com}}</ref> The Master Sword and Hylian Shield appear alongside Link's Champion's Tunic from ''Breath of the Wild'' as Amiibo-unlocked content in the Nintendo Switch version of ''[[The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McWhertor |first=Michael |date=2017-06-12 |title=Skyrim for Nintendo Switch has a little Legend of Zelda in it |url=https://www.polygon.com/e3/2017/6/12/15780168/skyrim-switch-e3-2017-bethesda-the-elder-scrolls-v-legend-of-zelda |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404074632/https://www.polygon.com/e3/2017/6/12/15780168/skyrim-switch-e3-2017-bethesda-the-elder-scrolls-v-legend-of-zelda |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-10-15 |website=Polygon}}</ref> In 2019, Link was added as a playable character in the 2.0.0 update to ''[[Super Mario Maker 2]]'', which features the Master Sword power-up changing the Mario characters into Link.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Shea |first=Brian |title=Super Mario Maker 2 Adds Link As A Playable Character |url=https://www.gameinformer.com/2019/12/02/super-mario-maker-2-adds-link-as-a-playable-character |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404100910/https://www.gameinformer.com/2019/12/02/super-mario-maker-2-adds-link-as-a-playable-character |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2023-10-30 |url-status=live |magazine=Game Informer}}</ref>

=== Television series ===
From 1989 to 1990, [[The Legend of Zelda (animated series)|''The Legend of Zelda'' animated series]] aired as a part of [[DIC Entertainment|DIC]]'s ''[[The Super Mario Bros. Super Show!]]''. Thirteen episodes were produced before the cancellation of ''The Super Mario Bros. Super Show''. Based loosely on the first game, the series presents Link as a rude, lovesick teenager. The plot revolves around him living in Hyrule Castle and being recruited to protect the Triforce of Wisdom from Ganon while accompanied by a fairy princess named Spryte. He persistently attempts to kiss Zelda and repeatedly exclaims "Well excuse me, Princess!" when tired with her attitude, a catchphrase that has since become a common gamers' [[in-joke]].<ref name=":13">{{Cite web |last=Moyse |first=Chris |date=2021-02-20 |title=I won't excuUUUuse Nintendo for The Legend of Zelda's animated antics |url=https://www.destructoid.com/i-wont-excuuuuuse-nintendo-for-the-legend-of-zeldas-animated-antics/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230701132922/https://www.destructoid.com/i-wont-excuuuuuse-nintendo-for-the-legend-of-zeldas-animated-antics/ |archive-date=1 July 2023 |access-date=2021-10-15 |website=Destructoid}}</ref><ref name=":14">{{cite web|author=Michael S. Drucker|date=2005-09-30|title=The Legend of Zelda: The Complete Animated Series|url=http://dvd.ign.com/articles/655/655002p1.html|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061006074445/http://dvd.ign.com/articles/655/655002p1.html|archive-date=2006-10-06|access-date=2006-09-29|website=IGN}}</ref> With little input from Nintendo, the writers were given the freedom to develop the series. As Link's first voice actor, Jonathan Potts pictured him as a, "youthful, goofy teenage boy who acts before he thinks".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Carpenter |first=Nicole |date=2023-01-11 |title='Excuuuuse me, Princess!': An oral history of The Legend of Zelda cartoon |url=https://www.polygon.com/zelda/23540526/legend-of-zelda-cartoon-oral-history-zeldathon |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230919234530/https://www.polygon.com/zelda/23540526/legend-of-zelda-cartoon-oral-history-zeldathon |archive-date=19 September 2023 |access-date=2023-04-01 |website=Polygon}}</ref>

A similar version of Link and Zelda appear during the second season of ''[[Captain N: The Game Master]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2017-07-15 |title=The Adaptation: The disasterpiece that was Captain N |url=https://www.destructoid.com/the-adaptation-the-disasterpiece-that-was-captain-n/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404114937/https://www.destructoid.com/the-adaptation-the-disasterpiece-that-was-captain-n/ |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-10-15 |website=Destructoid}}</ref> The storyline involves a character named Kevin being sucked into a TV and entering a game world. He helps Link and Zelda to stop Ganon from reviving to protect the Triforce.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-10-28 |title=Legend Of Zelda: Every Side Game And Spin Off |url=https://www.thegamer.com/legend-zelda-side-games-spin-off/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230603123525/https://www.thegamer.com/legend-zelda-side-games-spin-off/ |archive-date=3 June 2023 |access-date=2022-01-10 |website=TheGamer}}</ref>

Throughout 2013 and 2014, Link appeared in a series of comedic shorts titled ''The Legend of Zelda: The Misadventures of Link''. The series made its debut on ''[[Nintendo Video]]'', a [[video on demand]] service for the Nintendo 3DS. The series was based on ''The Wind Waker HD'' and aimed to present Link "in a new and hysterical light".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yoon |first=Andrew |date=31 October 2013 |title=The Legend of Zelda: The Misadventures of Link shows off our hero in 'new and hysterical light' |url=https://www.shacknews.com/article/81831/the-legend-of-zelda-the-misadventures-of-link-shows-off |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404224158/https://www.shacknews.com/article/81831/the-legend-of-zelda-the-misadventures-of-link-shows-off |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-01-27 |website=Shacknews}}</ref>

=== Film ===
Link is set to be portrayed by [[Benjamin Evan Ainsworth]] in the [[The Legend of Zelda (film)|live-action film]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Phillips |first=Tom |date=2025-07-16 |title=Nintendo Confirms Actors Set to Play Link and Zelda in Live-Action The Legend of Zelda Film |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/nintendo-confirms-actors-set-to-play-link-and-zelda-in-live-action-the-legend-of-zelda-film |access-date=2025-07-16 |website=IGN |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250716112449/https://www.ign.com/articles/nintendo-confirms-actors-set-to-play-link-and-zelda-in-live-action-the-legend-of-zelda-film |archive-date=2025-07-16 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Comics and manga ===
{{main|The Legend of Zelda (manga)}}

Following the release of the original game, Wanpaku Comics released a [[manga]] book in Japan in September 1986 titled "The Hyrule Fantasy". It follows the basic plot of the game with some additions, including Link and Zelda communicating telepathically.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McClain |first=J.K. |date=2017-08-21 |title=Video: Take a Detailed Look at the Original "Legend of Zelda" Manga |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2017/08/video_take_a_detailed_look_at_the_original_legend_of_zelda_manga |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231003210321/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2017/08/video_take_a_detailed_look_at_the_original_legend_of_zelda_manga |archive-date=3 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-26 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> Link appeared in manga-style novels published by [[Futabasha]], including ''The Legend of Zelda: The Mirage Castle'', which was published in Japanese in 1986.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gray |first=Kate |date=2022-01-28 |title=Random: Check Out This 1986 Choose-Your-Own-Adventure Book Where You Play As Zelda |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2022/01/random-check-out-this-1986-choose-your-own-adventure-book-where-you-play-as-zelda |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230529193321/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2022/01/random-check-out-this-1986-choose-your-own-adventure-book-where-you-play-as-zelda |archive-date=29 May 2023 |access-date=2022-02-02 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> A similar book titled ''The Legend of Zelda: The Triforce of the Gods'' was released in 1992 with a storyline based on ''A Link to the Past''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hagues |first=Alana |date=2022-05-23 |title=Random: The Final Zelda Choose-Your-Own-Adventure Book Has Been Uncovered |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2022/05/random-the-final-zelda-choose-your-own-adventure-book-has-been-uncovered |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230528135813/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2022/05/random-the-final-zelda-choose-your-own-adventure-book-has-been-uncovered |archive-date=28 May 2023 |access-date=2022-05-25 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> In the early 1990s, [[Valiant Comics]] published a serial comic based on ''The Legend of Zelda'' series.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Carpenter |first=Nicole |date=2023-03-28 |title=Remember the time when Link turned into Ganon in The Legend of Zelda? |url=https://www.polygon.com/zelda/23645381/legend-of-zelda-comic-book-valiant-ridiculous-moments |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230603020639/https://www.polygon.com/zelda/23645381/legend-of-zelda-comic-book-valiant-ridiculous-moments |archive-date=3 June 2023 |access-date=2023-03-31 |website=Polygon}}</ref> The comic series lasted for only five issues, with a sixth story published as part of the [[Nintendo Comics System]]. In this incarnation, Link's appearance was based on the original video game, giving him red hair, instead of his typical blonde or brown hair. The storyline reflects ''The Legend of Zelda'' animated television series, which was being aired around the same time. Link and Zelda's relationship is depicted as amorous, with Link attempting to get a kiss from Zelda and Zelda returning Link's affections.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ganguly |first=Srijani |date=2020-12-05 |title=Zelda: Everything You Didn't Know About The Valiant Comics Series |url=https://www.cbr.com/zelda-trivia-facts-valiant-comics-series/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404114938/https://www.cbr.com/zelda-trivia-facts-valiant-comics-series/ |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-11-12 |website=CBR}}</ref> A manga adaptation of ''A Link to the Past'' was created by Ataru Cagiva in 1995 with the Japanese title ''Triforce of the Gods''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Zelda no Densetsu: Kamigami no Triforce (manga, Ataru Cagiva) - Anime News Network |url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/encyclopedia/manga.php?id=9216 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230914220038/https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/encyclopedia/manga.php?id=9216 |archive-date=14 September 2023 |access-date=2023-09-26 |website=Anime News Network}}</ref> A serial comic was created for ''[[Nintendo Power]]'' magazine by Japanese manga artist [[Shotaro Ishinomori]]. It was published in 1992 and later collected in graphic novel form in 1993. The plot is an alternate version of the storyline from ''A Link to the Past''. Several other manga adaptations have been published by [[Viz Media]] based on the ''Zelda'' video games, including ''Ocarina of Time'', ''Majora's Mask'', ''A Link to the Past'' and ''The Minish Cap'', which were illustrated by [[Akira Himekawa]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=McWhertor |first=Michael |date=2015-01-26 |title=The Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past comic returns to print after more than 20 years |url=https://www.polygon.com/2015/1/26/7918091/legend-of-zelda-link-to-the-past-shotaro-ishinomori-manga |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231012053959/https://www.polygon.com/2015/1/26/7918091/legend-of-zelda-link-to-the-past-shotaro-ishinomori-manga |archive-date=12 October 2023 |access-date=2021-10-15 |website=Polygon}}</ref> The Japanese artist duo also wrote the long-running [[The Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess (manga)|''Twilight Princess'' manga]], which began as a Japanese serialisation in 2016 followed by an English translation in 2017 and has been released as a series of volumes.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tu |first=Trumann |date=2021-12-31 |title=Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess Manga Will Be Releasing Its Final Chapter |url=https://gamerant.com/legend-of-zelda-twilight-princess-manga-final-chapter/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404185431/https://gamerant.com/legend-of-zelda-twilight-princess-manga-final-chapter/ |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-01-01 |website=Game Rant}}</ref> Nintendo published ''Link's Hijinks'', a translated [[webcomic]] by Saitaro Komatsu on its Play Nintendo website. The comic had originally appeared in the Japanese manga magazine ''[[CoroCoro Comic]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Whitehead |first=Thomas |date=2015-08-08 |title=Nintendo Localises Saitaro Komatsu's "Link's Hijinks" Zelda Comic |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/08/nintendo_localises_saitaro_komatsus_links_hijinks_zelda_comic |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404183350/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/08/nintendo_localises_saitaro_komatsus_links_hijinks_zelda_comic |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-04-17 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref>

=== Gamebooks ===
Nintendo published several [[Nintendo gamebooks|gamebooks]] based on ''The Legend of Zelda'' series. As part of the ''Nintendo Adventure Book'' series, which was similar to the ''[[Choose Your Own Adventure]]'' books, two books were published in 1992 titled ''The Crystal Trap'' and ''The Shadow Prince''. In ''The Crystal Trap'', the storyline involves Zelda saving Link from the trap, while in ''The Shadow Prince'', Link must save Zelda with the help of a character named Charles. In 2001, two books based on ''[[The Legend of Zelda: Oracle of Seasons and Oracle of Ages|Oracle of Seasons and Oracle of Ages]]'' were published by [[Scholastic Corporation|Scholastic]], which were written by Craig Wessel and feature Link in a storyline based on the video games.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Hilliard|first=Kyle|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pkN4DgAAQBAJ&dq=the+crystal+trap+nintendo+book&pg=PT124|title=The Legendary World of Zelda|date=2017-05-01|publisher=Triumph Books|isbn=978-1-63319-818-0|access-date=2022-01-22|archive-date=2024-03-08|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240308213403/https://books.google.com/books?id=pkN4DgAAQBAJ&dq=the+crystal+trap+nintendo+book&pg=PT124#v=onepage&q=the%20crystal%20trap%20nintendo%20book&f=false|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Merchandise ==
Since 2014, various [[amiibo]] of Link have been released in a variety of incarnations. Three amiibo for the ''Super Smash Bros.'' series of Link, Toon Link and Young Link were released in 2014, 2015 and 2019. Various incarnations have been created as amiibo for the ''Zelda'' series, including Wolf Link and Midna, 8-bit Link, ''Ocarina of Time'' Link, Toon Link from ''The Wind Waker'', incarnations from ''Majora's Mask'', ''Skyward Sword'', ''Twilight Princess'' and ''Link's Awakening'', archer and rider figures from ''Breath of the Wild'' and the incarnation from ''Tears of the Kingdom''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Macy |first=Seth G. |date=2023-05-11 |title=Here's Every Legend of Zelda Amiibo |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/heres-every-legend-of-zelda-amiibo |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023214900/https://www.ign.com/articles/heres-every-legend-of-zelda-amiibo |archive-date=23 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-18 |website=IGN}}</ref> ''[[Guinness World Records]]'' reported that as of 1 February 2018, Link is the most prolific Nintendo character released as an amiibo.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 January 2017 |title=Most prolific amiibo name |url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/512700-most-prolific-amiibo-name |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404122703/https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/512700-most-prolific-amiibo-name |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=17 November 2021 |website=Guinness World Records}}</ref> In addition to appearing on branded merchandise, his image has been reproduced in the form of figurines, plush toys and action figures.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lobley |first=William |date=2021-02-15 |title=The Best Zelda Merchandise |url=https://www.empireonline.com/shopping/gaming/best-zelda-merchandise/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230713051817/https://www.empireonline.com/shopping/gaming/best-zelda-merchandise/ |archive-date=13 July 2023 |access-date=2023-09-18 |website=Empire}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Whitehead |first=Thomas |date=2015-02-14 |title=This Articulated Skyward Sword Link Toy Should be Worth a Lot of Rupees |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/02/this_articulated_skyward_sword_link_toy_should_be_worth_a_lot_of_rupees |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029181758/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/02/this_articulated_skyward_sword_link_toy_should_be_worth_a_lot_of_rupees |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> In May 2024, several versions of Link were announced in [[Lego minifigure]] form for a two-in-one Lego set of The Great Deku Tree, including adult and young Link from ''Ocarina of Time'' and Link from ''Breath of the Wild''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Parrish |first=Ash |date=2024-05-28 |title=Lego's first Legend of Zelda set is a 2,500-piece Great Deku Tree |url=https://www.theverge.com/2024/5/28/24166162/lego-legend-of-zelda-set-preorder-great-deku-tree |access-date=2024-06-03 |website=The Verge |language=en}}</ref>

== Reception ==
=== Critical reception ===
Link has been widely described by critics as one of the greatest or most iconic video game characters of all time.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Whitaker |first=Ron |date=2015-04-03 |title=8 of the Most Iconic Video Game Heroes |url=https://www.escapistmagazine.com/8-of-the-most-iconic-video-game-heroes/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132939/https://www.escapistmagazine.com/8-of-the-most-iconic-video-game-heroes/ |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-18 |website=The Escapist}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite web |date=2010-11-06 |title=The 50 Greatest Video Game Characters |url=https://www.empireonline.com/movies/features/50-greatest-video-game-characters/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230718014115/https://www.empireonline.com/movies/features/50-greatest-video-game-characters/ |archive-date=18 July 2023 |access-date=2023-09-18 |website=Empire}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Weber |first=Rachel |date=2022-06-25 |title=The 25 best heroes in games of all-time |url=https://www.gamesradar.com/heroes-in-video-games/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230531160620/https://www.gamesradar.com/heroes-in-video-games/ |archive-date=31 May 2023 |access-date=2023-04-04 |website=GamesRadar+}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-03-09 |title=20 greatest video game characters ranked, from Lara Croft to Sonic the Hedgehog |first=James |last=McMahon |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/games/best-video-game-characters-mario-sonic-the-hedgehog-red-dead-redemption-last-of-us-a9355406.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230929103259/https://www.independent.co.uk/games/best-video-game-characters-mario-sonic-the-hedgehog-red-dead-redemption-last-of-us-a9355406.html |archive-date=29 September 2023 |access-date=2023-09-18 |website=The Independent}}</ref> Gabriel Aikins of ''[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]]'' noted that he is one of the most recognisable and beloved characters in the gaming industry.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Aikins |first=Gabriel |date=2023-04-22 |title=Which of The Legend of Zelda's Links would be the best hangs? |url=https://www.polygon.com/zelda/23692483/legend-of-zelda-link-wind-waker-ocarina-of-time-majoras-mask |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230727130757/https://www.polygon.com/zelda/23692483/legend-of-zelda-link-wind-waker-ocarina-of-time-majoras-mask |archive-date=27 July 2023 |access-date=2023-09-24 |website=Polygon}}</ref> ''[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]'' described him as one of the "most badass" comic book characters,<ref>Drea Avellan, [http://www.complex.com/video-games/2013/01/the-50-most-badass-video-game-characters-of-all-time/ The 50 Most Badass comic book Characters Of All Time] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150219033800/http://www.complex.com/pop-culture/2013/02/the-50-most-badass-video-game-characters-of-all-time/|date=2015-02-19}}, Complex.com, February 1, 2013.</ref> and emphasised the fond memories that most gamers have of their first encounter with Link and the nostalgia that he creates within the video game community.<ref name="25mascots"/> ''[[UGO]]'' considered Link to be the top comic book character who needs their own movie: "Get [[Peter Jackson]] or [[Guillermo del Toro]] to do it, dump a ton of money into it, and we'll all die happy".<ref>{{cite web|last=Meli |first=Marissa |url=http://www.ugo.com/games/paging-uwe-boll-video-game-characters-who-need-their-own-movies?page=3 |title=comic book Characters&nbsp;– comic book Movies |work=UGO.com |date=2011-07-19 |access-date=2013-07-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110829103359/http://www.ugo.com/games/paging-uwe-boll-video-game-characters-who-need-their-own-movies?page=3 |archive-date=2011-08-29}}</ref> Link has also proven to be a popular guest character in other video games series. In 2008, ''IGN'' chose him as the best bonus character in the ''[[Soulcalibur]]'' series,<ref>{{cite web |last=Schedeen |first=Jesse |date=2008-07-31 |title=Soulcalibur: Top Bonus Characters – Stars Feature at IGN |url=http://uk.stars.ign.com/articles/895/895519p8.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220816014131/https://www.ign.com/articles/2008/07/31/soulcalibur-top-bonus-characters?page=8 |archive-date=16 August 2022 |access-date=2013-07-30 |website=IGN |publisher=}}</ref> while ''[[GameDaily]]'' considered him the best ''Smash Bros.'' character.<ref>{{cite web |title=Top 10 Smash Bros. Characters – Page 10 |url=http://www.gamedaily.com/articles/galleries/top-ten-super-smash-bros-characters/?page=10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080701052548/http://www.gamedaily.com/articles/galleries/top-ten-super-smash-bros-characters/?page=10 |archive-date=2008-07-01 |publisher=[[GameDaily]] |access-date=2009-08-07}}</ref> Link's acquisition of the Master Sword has been described as an "epic" series-defining moment<ref>{{Cite web |last=Norman |first=Jim |date=2023-07-10 |title=Soapbox: Getting The Master Sword In Tears Of The Kingdom Is Zelda At Its Best |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/features/soapbox-getting-the-master-sword-in-tears-of-the-kingdom-is-zelda-at-its-best |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230802075300/https://www.nintendolife.com/features/soapbox-getting-the-master-sword-in-tears-of-the-kingdom-is-zelda-at-its-best |archive-date=2 August 2023 |access-date=2023-09-27 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> and the sequence in ''Ocarina of Time'' was ranked as the most unforgettable moment in gaming by ''[[IGN]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Top 100 Unforgettable Video Game Moments |url=https://www.ign.com/lists/top-100-video-game-moments |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601235340/https://www.ign.com/lists/top-100-video-game-moments |archive-date=1 June 2023 |website=IGN}}</ref> Matthew Byrd, writing for ''[[Den of Geek]]'', said that it represents the transformative moment for both Link and the player when he finally achieves his destiny as hero.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Byrd |first=Matthew |date=2022-04-13 |title=Why A Link to the Past Is the Definitive Legend of Zelda Game |url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/a-link-to-the-past-the-legend-of-zelda-retrospective-definitive-game/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230802060657/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/a-link-to-the-past-the-legend-of-zelda-retrospective-definitive-game/ |archive-date=2 August 2023 |access-date=2023-09-27 |website=Den of Geek}}</ref> Link was chosen as the "hottest video game character" in June 2006 by ''[[Out (magazine)|Out]]'' magazine for appearing as "Nintendo's sexy farm-boy-turned-wolf" in ''Twilight Princess''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gay mag "Hot" for Link |url=https://www.gamespot.com/articles/gay-mag-hot-for-link/1100-6151905/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230318022548/https://www.gamespot.com/articles/gay-mag-hot-for-link/1100-6151905/ |archive-date=18 March 2023 |access-date=2021-10-12 |website=GameSpot}}</ref> Critics have commented on his sex appeal, including Jeffrey Parkes of ''Polygon'' who likened his ''Skyward Sword'' incarnation to a [[himbo]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Parkin |first=Jeffrey |date=2021-07-15 |title=Link is the ideal himbo of video games |url=https://www.polygon.com/22578543/zelda-skyward-sword-nintendo-switch-link-himbo |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132942/https://www.polygon.com/22578543/zelda-skyward-sword-nintendo-switch-link-himbo |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-21 |website=Polygon}}</ref> and Kirsten Carey of ''[[The Mary Sue]]'' who described his official ''Tears of the Kingdom'' artwork as "a dangerous [[thirst trap]]".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Carey |first=Kirsten |date=2023-02-10 |title=New Artwork Of Link In 'The Legend Of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom' Is A Dangerous Thirst Trap |url=https://www.themarysue.com/new-artwork-of-link-in-the-legend-of-zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-is-a-dangerous-thirst-trap/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230603044842/https://www.themarysue.com/new-artwork-of-link-in-the-legend-of-zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-is-a-dangerous-thirst-trap/ |archive-date=3 June 2023 |access-date=2023-09-20 |website=The Mary Sue}}</ref> Brian Feldman for ''[[New York (magazine)|New York]]'' magazine highlighted that Nintendo had finally acknowledged Link's sex appeal through the dialogue of various side characters in ''Breath of the Wild''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Feldman |first=Brian |date=15 March 2017 |title=Everyone Is Thirsty for Link From The Legend of Zelda |url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2017/03/everyone-is-thirsty-for-link-from-the-legend-of-zelda.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404183301/https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2017/03/everyone-is-thirsty-for-link-from-the-legend-of-zelda.html |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-10-12 |website=New York magazine}}</ref>

Anthony John Agnello of ''[[GamesRadar+]]'', described Link's earliest iteration as lacking in height and expressiveness, but impressively fast and versatile for an RPG hero. He noted that his design was upgraded to a "gloriously personable cartoon character" by the release of ''A Link to the Past''.<ref name="EvolutionRadar" /> The cartoon rendition in the 1989 television series was widely criticised, partially due to its obnoxious characterisation of the hero that Michael Mammano of ''Den of Geek'' described as "appalling".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mammano |first=Michael |date=2018-04-06 |title=The Legend of Zelda Animated Series: A Very Strange History |url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-animated-series-a-very-strange-history/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230725000010/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-animated-series-a-very-strange-history/ |archive-date=25 July 2023 |access-date=2023-09-25 |website=Den of Geek}}</ref><ref name=":13" /><ref name=":14" /> The child iteration in ''Ocarina of Time'' was fondly remembered by Dominic Preston writing for ''[[NME]]'', who noted the clever touches in his character that help players to see the game through a child's eyes and motivate them to explore.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Preston |first=Dominic |date=2021-07-27 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time's secret strength has always been Hyrule |url=https://www.nme.com/features/gaming-features/the-legend-of-zelda-ocarina-of-times-secret-strength-has-always-been-hyrule-3003504 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230719035544/https://www.nme.com/features/gaming-features/the-legend-of-zelda-ocarina-of-times-secret-strength-has-always-been-hyrule-3003504 |archive-date=19 July 2023 |access-date=2023-09-22 |website=NME}}</ref> Richard George and Audrey Drake of ''IGN'' commented that the game was the first to give Link more character depth and used its time shifts to illustrate his growth from youthful innocence to adulthood.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Drake |first=Richard George and Audrey |date=2011-04-30 |title=Hyrule Times: The Impact of Ocarina of Time |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/04/30/hyrule-times-the-impact-of-ocarina-of-time |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132939/https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/04/30/hyrule-times-the-impact-of-ocarina-of-time |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-22 |website=IGN}}</ref> Ryan Lambie of ''[[Den of Geek]]'' described Link's combat abilities in the game as "revolutionary", particularly the Z-targeting system that allowed him to lock on and face his enemies in swordplay, which was afterwards adopted throughout the games industry.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lambie |first=Ryan |date=2018-02-21 |title=The Many Innovations of The Legend of Zelda |url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-many-innovations-of-the-legend-of-zelda/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230429192853/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-many-innovations-of-the-legend-of-zelda/ |archive-date=29 April 2023 |access-date=2023-09-22 |website=Den of Geek}}</ref> According to Lotte Reinbold, writing for ''[[The Guardian]]'', his transformations into various creatures in ''Majora's Mask'' were "shocking and profound", as the pain he experiences when putting on a mask seems to strip away at his former identity as the Hero of Time.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reinbold |first=Lotte |date=2020-05-30 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Majora's Mask at 20 – still eerie and profound |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/games/2020/may/30/the-legend-of-zelda-majoras-mask-at-20-still-eerie-and-profound |access-date=2023-09-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230614045532/https://www.theguardian.com/games/2020/may/30/the-legend-of-zelda-majoras-mask-at-20-still-eerie-and-profound |archive-date=14 June 2023 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Kate Gray for ''[[Nintendo Life]]'' highlighted Link's stoicism and altruism in the face of imminent death and opined that the game centres on his sacrifice and resulting loss of innocence and childhood.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gray |first=Kate |date=2021-03-13 |title=Zelda: Majora's Mask Is A Testament To What Nintendo Is Capable Of When It Gets Weird |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2021/03/feature_zelda_majoras_mask_is_a_testament_to_what_nintendo_is_capable_of_when_it_gets_weird |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230926112425/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2021/03/feature_zelda_majoras_mask_is_a_testament_to_what_nintendo_is_capable_of_when_it_gets_weird |archive-date=26 September 2023 |access-date=2023-09-26 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref>

[[File:Wakerlink.jpg|thumb|upright|alt=A cartoon version of Link wearing a green tunic and cap and holding a magical baton |Toon Link's unveiling for ''[[The Wind Waker]]'' proved to be initially controversial due to its highly stylised cartoon design.<ref name=":3" /><ref name=ign1/>]]
When Miyamoto unveiled the cartoon design for Link at [[E3 2002]] for ''[[The Wind Waker]]'', it diverged from a more realistic young adult model previously seen battling [[Ganondorf]] at the Nintendo Space World Demo in 2000. ''IGN'' noted that the crowd did not respond with the same enthusiasm that was seen at the unveiling of ''Twilight Princess'' in 2004.<ref name=":7" /> Toon Link was presented at a time when the games industry was pushing for grittier heroes. Many fans responded with outrage after seeing Link depicted in a childlike cartoonish style. Aonuma commented that this response was due to gamers fearing change, as the art style was so different from previous games in the series. The reception to Toon Link later improved, leading many players to express a fondness for the character.<ref name=":3" /> Jose Otero of ''IGN'' commented, "Of all the incarnations of Link in the 3D era of this series, the young, cartoon iteration is the most capable fighter".<ref name=ign1>{{Cite web |last=Otero |first=Jose |date=2013-09-17 |title=The Legend of Zelda: The Wind Waker HD Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2013/09/17/the-legend-of-zelda-the-wind-waker-hd-review |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231014092319/https://www.ign.com/articles/2013/09/17/the-legend-of-zelda-the-wind-waker-hd-review |archive-date=14 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-20 |website=IGN}}</ref> In his review of ''[[The Wind Waker HD]]'', Oli Welsh of ''[[Eurogamer]]'' said this was his favourite Link, describing him and the game as "energetic, spirited and soulful".<ref name="EuroHD" />

David Meikleham of ''GamesRadar+'' noted that following the controversy around Toon Link, Nintendo had played it safe by unveiling the most graphically realistic version of Link created for ''Twilight Princess'' and that this incarnation was warmly received by fans who wanted to see a handsome elfin hero.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Meikleham |first=David |date=2023-04-20 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess marked the end of the GameCube era and the dawn of the Wii |url=https://www.gamesradar.com/the-legend-of-zelda-twilight-princess-marked-the-end-of-the-gamecube-era-and-the-dawn-of-the-wii/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230605040251/https://www.gamesradar.com/the-legend-of-zelda-twilight-princess-marked-the-end-of-the-gamecube-era-and-the-dawn-of-the-wii/ |archive-date=5 June 2023 |access-date=2023-09-22 |website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> When a preview trailer depicting Link in the realistic art style was unveiled at E3 2004, the audience reacted with frenzied applause.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Klepek |first=Patrick |date=2016-02-22 |title=That Time A Zelda Trailer Supposedly Made Grown Men Cry |url=https://kotaku.com/that-time-a-zelda-trailer-supposedly-made-grown-men-cry-1760602838 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132938/https://kotaku.com/that-time-a-zelda-trailer-supposedly-made-grown-men-cry-1760602838 |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-29 |website=Kotaku}}</ref> Griffin McElfroy of ''Polygon'' felt that Link's transition into Wolf Link did not provide satisfying combat when compared to the mastery of his sword manoeuvres.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McElroy |first=Griffin |date=2016-03-01 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess HD review |url=https://www.polygon.com/2016/3/1/11137784/the-legend-of-zelda-twilight-princess-hd-review |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230606192249/https://www.polygon.com/2016/3/1/11137784/the-legend-of-zelda-twilight-princess-hd-review |archive-date=6 June 2023 |access-date=2023-09-23 |website=Polygon}}</ref> Oli Welsh thought that Link's inseparable relationship with Zelda in ''Spirit Tracks'' was "adorable" and "heartwarming", as it changed the convention of Link being a lone hero.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Welsh |first=Oli |date=2009-12-08 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Spirit Tracks |work=Eurogamer.net |url=https://www.eurogamer.net/the-legend-of-zelda-spirit-tracks-review |access-date=2023-09-26 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206221142/https://www.eurogamer.net/the-legend-of-zelda-spirit-tracks-review |archive-date=6 February 2023}}</ref> The relationship between Link and Zelda in ''Skyward Sword'' was praised by Susan Arendt of ''[[The Escapist (magazine)|The Escapist]]'' for moving away from the traditional narrative of a prophesied hero to a more personal concern of saving his childhood friend.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arendt |first=Susan |date=2011-11-11 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Skyward Sword Review |url=https://www.escapistmagazine.com/the-legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-review/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230322160740/https://www.escapistmagazine.com/the-legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-review/ |archive-date=22 March 2023 |access-date=2023-09-23 |website=The Escapist}}</ref> In his review of ''Skyward Sword HD'', Josh West of ''GamesRadar+'' felt that Link looks "a little ridiculous" holding up his sword as he traverses the game environment resulting from Nintendo's decision to centre the game around motion controls, but considered it a small price for the precision of the gameplay.<ref>{{Cite web |last=West |first=Josh |date=2021-07-14 |title=Legend of Zelda: Skyward Sword HD review: "One of the most misunderstood games in the series gets a second chance" |url=https://www.gamesradar.com/legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-hd-review/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230724230716/https://www.gamesradar.com/legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-hd-review/ |archive-date=24 July 2023 |access-date=2023-09-24 |website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> In an ''IGN'' review of ''A Link Between Worlds'', Keza Macdonald opined that the game world had less personality than others in the series due to Link being reduced to a blank character, but commented that the nostalgia for ''A Link to the Past'' was "emotionally potent".<ref>{{Cite web |last=MacDonald |first=Keza |date=2013-11-14 |title=The Legend of Zelda: A Link Between Worlds Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2013/11/14/the-legend-of-zelda-a-link-between-worlds-review |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230407021057/https://www.ign.com/articles/2013/11/14/the-legend-of-zelda-a-link-between-worlds-review |archive-date=7 April 2023 |access-date=2023-09-26 |website=IGN}}</ref>

Jacob Krastenakes of ''[[The Verge]]'', questioned Link's role as the player character and opined that he and Zelda could be switched and "he could simply be the ''other'' character, who is off doing their own thing".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kastrenakes |first=Jacob |date=2016-06-14 |title=Nintendo offers really stupid reasons why you can't play as a woman in The Legend of Zelda |url=https://www.theverge.com/2016/6/14/11939552/nintendo-has-dumb-reasons-for-not-making-a-link-a-woman |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602172322/https://www.theverge.com/2016/6/14/11939552/nintendo-has-dumb-reasons-for-not-making-a-link-a-woman |archive-date=2 June 2023 |access-date=2021-10-12 |website=The Verge}}</ref> Ben Kuchera of ''Polygon'' noted that although Nintendo "can bend the lore and mythology to justify every other goofy idea" they draw the line at a female version of the character.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kuchera |first=Ben |date=2016-06-14 |title=The argument against Link being a woman, destroyed with one image |url=https://www.polygon.com/e3/2016/6/14/11939308/zelda-nintendo-bad-excuses |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230929102822/https://www.polygon.com/e3/2016/6/14/11939308/zelda-nintendo-bad-excuses |archive-date=29 September 2023 |access-date=2023-09-18 |website=Polygon}}</ref> Sara Gitkos for ''[[iMore]]'' questioned Link's role as the protagonist, noting that the titular character of the series had been sidelined. She commented that Zelda served as Link's prize, although in recent games she is more of a partner, but conceded that this would be difficult to change because Link "doesn't have any character to speak of, but rather is the place holder for us, and we become him".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gitkos |first=Sara |date=2021-02-18 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Why she should be the hero |url=https://www.imore.com/legend-zelda-why-she-should-be-hero |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404114938/https://www.imore.com/legend-zelda-why-she-should-be-hero |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-10-12 |website=iMore}}</ref>

Link's abilities in ''Breath of the Wild'' were lauded, with Matthew Castle of ''GamesRadar+'' calling his flexibility, particularly when climbing cliffs, a "revelation" compared to other game heroes.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Castle |first=Matthew |date=2017-03-02 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild review: "It's taken 18 years, but Nintendo has done it again: this is the adventure game to beat" |url=https://www.gamesradar.com/zelda-breath-of-the-wild-review/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230726155605/https://www.gamesradar.com/zelda-breath-of-the-wild-review/ |archive-date=26 July 2023 |access-date=2023-09-21 |website=GamesRadar+}}</ref> In his ''Nintendo Life'' review of the game, Thomas Whitehead praised his athleticism, particularly the ability to dash, climb and glide, for being essential components for problem solving.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Whitehead |first=Thomas |date=2017-03-02 |title=Review: The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild (Switch) |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/reviews/nintendo-switch/the_legend_of_zelda_breath_of_the_wild |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230531184924/https://www.nintendolife.com/reviews/nintendo-switch/the_legend_of_zelda_breath_of_the_wild |archive-date=31 May 2023 |access-date=2023-09-29 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> Matt Kamen of ''[[Wired (magazine)|Wired]]'' felt that his vulnerability, caused by starting the game in rags and collecting highly degradable weapons, impacts the gameplay by making the player more cautious.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Kamen |first=Matt |title=Zelda: Breath of the Wild review: an epic masterpiece |magazine=Wired UK |url=https://www.wired.co.uk/article/legend-zelda-breath-wild-nintendo-switch-review |access-date=2023-09-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230420013255/https://www.wired.co.uk/article/legend-zelda-breath-wild-nintendo-switch-review |archive-date=20 April 2023 |issn=1357-0978}}</ref> Anticipation was high leading up to the release of ''Tears of the Kingdom'', partially driven by speculation over Link's glowing right arm, which featured prominently in the trailers.<ref name=":11" /> This led to various theories about its role and implications in the story.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gray |first=Kate |date=2022-12-24 |title=16 Theories About Link's Weird Arm In Zelda: Breath Of The Wild 2 |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/features/16-theories-about-links-weird-arm-in-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-2 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230822171750/https://www.nintendolife.com/features/16-theories-about-links-weird-arm-in-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-2 |archive-date=22 August 2023 |access-date=2023-09-20 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> Isaiah Colbert of ''Kotaku'' noted the similarities of this scenario to Prince Ashitaka whose right arm is cursed in the 1997 Japanese animated film ''[[Princess Mononoke]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Colbert |first=Isaiah |date=2023-04-13 |title=Tears of the Kingdom Could Go Full Princess Mononoke With Zelda |url=https://kotaku.com/legend-of-zelda-tears-kingdom-princess-mononoke-anime-1850333639 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029161602/https://kotaku.com/legend-of-zelda-tears-kingdom-princess-mononoke-anime-1850333639 |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-20 |website=Kotaku}}</ref> Link's new abilities granted by his arm were praised by critics.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Erskine |first=Donovan |date=2023-04-26 |title=Ultrahand and Fuse are game changers in The Legend of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom |url=https://www.shacknews.com/article/135125/zelda-tears-of-the-kindom-hands-on-impressions |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029132941/https://www.shacknews.com/article/135125/zelda-tears-of-the-kindom-hands-on-impressions |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-22 |website=Shacknews}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Friscia |first=John |date=2023-03-28 |title=Tears of the Kingdom Gameplay Video Reveals Fuse, Ultrahand |url=https://www.escapistmagazine.com/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-gameplay-video-recall-fuse-ultrahand-ascend/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230609204303/https://www.escapistmagazine.com/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-gameplay-video-recall-fuse-ultrahand-ascend/ |archive-date=9 June 2023 |access-date=2023-09-22 |website=The Escapist}}</ref> Steve Watts of ''GameSpot'' commented that his abilities are the engine driving the game and are a "beautifully implemented evolution" providing opportunities for inventiveness and creativity.<ref name="gamespot watts"/> Ash Parrish of ''The Verge'' said that his combined powers "create infinite possibilities" but noted that he is "tasked with the same old quest: find Zelda".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Parrish |first=Ash |date=2023-05-11 |title=Tears of the Kingdom is a legend retold with slightly less wonder |url=https://www.theverge.com/23718926/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-review-nintendo-switch |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231030084631/https://www.theverge.com/23718926/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-review-nintendo-switch |archive-date=30 October 2023 |access-date=2023-09-21 |website=The Verge}}</ref>

=== Analysis ===
Jonathan Holmes, writing for ''[[Destructoid]]'', questioned whether Link should be viewed as an individual or a blank slate [[Avatar (computing)|avatar]]. He said that Nintendo had successfully created Link as both a specific character and an abstract concept, illustrated by the player choosing the character's name at the start of various games. Describing Link as a "non-character {{sic|wh|o's|expecting=whose}} only purpose is to act as doorway for the player into the game world", he opined that through the reincarnation myth and use of multiple timelines, Nintendo had successfully shaped Link into a series of individuals.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Holmes |first=Jonathan |date=2015-11-20 |title=Does it matter if Link is a boy or a girl? |url=https://www.destructoid.com/does-it-matter-if-link-is-a-boy-or-a-girl/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404104853/https://www.destructoid.com/does-it-matter-if-link-is-a-boy-or-a-girl/ |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2021-10-12 |website=Destructoid}}</ref> ''[[Den of Geek]]''{{'}}s Aaron Greenbaum asserted that even though Link does not have voice lines, he is a different character in every incarnation and each game makes it clear that he communicates: "This paradoxical characterization helps players insert themselves into Link's shoes but also gives him a personality, despite not being designed with one".<ref name=":9">{{Cite web |last=Greenbaum |first=Aaron |date=2022-05-06 |title=The Legend of Zelda: Why Link Never Speaks |url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-why-link-never-speaks/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230605034521/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-why-link-never-speaks/ |archive-date=5 June 2023 |access-date=2023-04-04 |website=Den of Geek}}</ref> Ryan Lambie of ''Den of Geek'' praised him as "the videogame equivalent of [[Tintin (character)|Tintin]]" because he is, "the archetypal young hero, embodying all the optimism, generosity and spirit of adventure a broad fantasy action game requires". He also praised Link as "the ultimate videogame [[underdog]]" and "an ordinary yet brave little guy who wants to save the world, in spite of insurmountable odds".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lambie |first=Ryan |date=2011-11-23 |title=The Legend Of Zelda: why Link is one of the most enduring characters in videogaming |url=https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-why-link-is-one-of-the-most-enduring-characters-in-videogaming/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220929012417/https://www.denofgeek.com/games/the-legend-of-zelda-why-link-is-one-of-the-most-enduring-characters-in-videogaming/ |archive-date=29 September 2022 |access-date=2021-10-12 |website=Den of Geek}}</ref> Riley McAtee, writing for ''[[The Ringer (website)|The Ringer]]'', opined that the spirit of Link is the combination of courage as a defining characteristic and the absence of dialogue, which help to immerse players in the game, making him "the quintessential video game hero".<ref>{{Cite news |last=McAtee |first=Riley |date=22 February 2021 |title=Character Study: Link, the Quiet Hero of 'The Legend of Zelda' |url=https://www.theringer.com/2021/2/22/22291859/link-legend-of-zelda-character-study |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602220026/https://www.theringer.com/2021/2/22/22291859/link-legend-of-zelda-character-study |archive-date=2 June 2023 |website=The Ringer}}</ref> Nick Thorpe of ''[[GamesRadar+]]'' considered that the success of Link's character as an archetypal hero is that players identify and grow with him during each game and that his accomplishments are a "shared victory" where the player can achieve great feats that could never be accomplished in reality.<ref name=":0" />

=== Influence and legacy ===
[[File:Link star, Walk of Game.JPG|left|thumb|alt=A metal star engraved with Link's name and ''The Legend of Zelda'' game title |Link's star at the [[Walk of Game]] in the [[Metreon]], [[San Francisco]]]]
As the [[List of video game mascots|mascot]] of ''The Legend of Zelda'' series, Link has become a widely recognisable character in [[pop culture]].<ref name="25mascots">{{Cite web |last=Turner |first=Gus |date=23 August 2013 |title=The Best Video Game Mascots of All Time |url=https://www.complex.com/pop-culture/a/rich-knight/best-video-game-mascots |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230811203528/https://www.complex.com/pop-culture/a/rich-knight/best-video-game-mascots |archive-date=11 August 2023 |access-date=2023-08-11 |website=Complex}}</ref> Damien McFerran of ''[[Nintendo Life]]'' remarked that the enigmatic nature of his character leaves him open to interpretation and makes him "one of the most drawn and imitated video game icons".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-11-30 |first=Damien |last=McFerran |title=Art: Halo Character Artist Gives Legend Of Zelda's Link A Realistic Makeover |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/11/art_halo_character_artist_gives_legend_of_zeldas_link_a_realistic_makeover |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230403232623/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2015/11/art_halo_character_artist_gives_legend_of_zeldas_link_a_realistic_makeover |archive-date=3 April 2023 |access-date=2023-04-03 |website=Nintendo Life}}</ref> In 2011, readers of the ''[[Guinness World Records Gamer's Edition]]'' voted Link as the second best video game character of all time, behind [[Mario]].<ref>{{cite web|date=February 16, 2011|title=Top 50 video game characters of all time announced in Guinness World Records 2011 Gamer's Edition|url=http://www.gamasutra.com/view/pressreleases/68759/TOP_50_VIDEO_GAME_CHARACTERS_OF_ALL_TIME_ANNOUNCED_inGUINNESS_WORLD_RECORDS_2011_GAMERrsquoS_EDITION.php|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303192336/http://www.gamasutra.com/view/pressreleases/68759/TOP_50_VIDEO_GAME_CHARACTERS_OF_ALL_TIME_ANNOUNCED_inGUINNESS_WORLD_RECORDS_2011_GAMERrsquoS_EDITION.php|archive-date=March 3, 2016|access-date=July 9, 2011|website=[[Gamasutra]]}}</ref> Since the release of ''Super Smash Bros. Ultimate'' in December 2018, he holds the ''Guinness World Record'' as the most ubiquitous action-adventure video game character, having appeared in 40 unique video games, not including rereleases and HD remasters.<ref>{{Cite web |date=7 December 2018 |title=Most ubiquitous action-adventure videogame character |url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/105396-most-prolific-action-adventure-videogame-character |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404233643/https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/105396-most-prolific-action-adventure-videogame-character |archive-date=4 April 2023 |access-date=2022-02-16 |website=Guinness World Records}}</ref> On 3 June 2025, ''Guinness World Records'' named Link as the most critically acclaimed videogame playable character, as he has appeared in nine unique entries in [[GameRankings.com]]'s list of the top 100 all-time best reviewed videogames.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 June 2025 |title=Most critically acclaimed videogame playable character |url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/545881-most-critically-acclaimed-videogame-playable-character |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250901092522/https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/545881-most-critically-acclaimed-videogame-playable-character |archive-date=1 September 2025 |access-date=4 October 2025 |website=Guinness World Records}}</ref>

Critics widely consider Link's adventures to be influential on the video game industry.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mahardy |first=Mike |date=19 February 2016 |title=The Legacy of Zelda |url=https://www.gamespot.com/articles/the-legacy-of-zelda/1100-6434921/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516114832/https://www.gamespot.com/articles/the-legacy-of-zelda/1100-6434921/ |archive-date=16 May 2023 |access-date=2023-09-23 |website=GameSpot}}</ref> The original ''The Legend of Zelda'' video game influenced the evolution of the action [[role-playing video game]] genre, being an early example of [[open world]] design and [[nonlinear gameplay]]. The series has inspired numerous other game titles to adopt a similar action-adventure gameplay and open world exploration, with these games being commonly referred to as "''Zelda'' clones".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Finnegan |first=Liz |date=2016-02-27 |title=How The Legend of Zelda Changed Video Games |url=https://www.escapistmagazine.com/how-the-legend-of-zelda-changed-video-games/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230720033822/https://www.escapistmagazine.com/how-the-legend-of-zelda-changed-video-games/ |archive-date=20 July 2023 |access-date=2023-04-02 |website=The Escapist}}</ref><ref name="35yearszelda"/> In 2005, Link was awarded a star on the [[Walk of Game]] along with his creator, [[Shigeru Miyamoto]], noting that "the series has produced the most popular role-playing game titles in history".<ref name="walk">{{cite web|title=Past Inductees|url=http://www.walkofgame.com/inductees/inductees2005.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080121064109/http://www.walkofgame.com/inductees/inductees2005.html|archive-date=2008-01-21|access-date=2007-12-20|work=WalkOfGame.com|publisher=[[Metreon]]}}</ref> In 2011, ''[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]]'' said that "Link has grown into one of the world's most celebrated console heroes, and helped establish [[Rpgs|RPGs]] as an international gaming standard".<ref name=":2" /> In 2017, ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' named Link as one of the most influential video game characters of all time commenting: "Link embodies the selfless hero on a transformative journey, a storytelling trope we've seen in countless titles from ''Mass Effect''{{'}}s [[Commander Shepard]] to ''Halo''{{'}}s [[Master Chief (Halo)|Master Chief]]".<ref>{{Cite magazine |date=2017-06-30 |title=The 15 Most Influential Video Game Characters of All Time |url=https://time.com/4836507/influential-video-game-characters/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230130081443/https://time.com/4836507/influential-video-game-characters/ |archive-date=30 January 2023 |access-date=2023-10-30 |magazine=Time}}</ref> In 2024, a poll conducted by [[British Academy of Film and Television Arts|BAFTA]] with around 4,000 respondents named Link as the seventh most iconic video-game character of all time.<ref>{{Cite news|author=Ollie Reynolds|title=Random: BAFTA's 'Iconic Game Characters' Poll Has Us Scratching Our Heads|url=https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2024/04/random-baftas-iconic-game-characters-poll-has-us-scratching-our-heads|work=Nintendo Life|date=April 3, 2024|accessdate=April 8, 2024|archive-date=April 4, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240404102422/https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2024/04/random-baftas-iconic-game-characters-poll-has-us-scratching-our-heads|url-status=live}}</ref>

